window.CANOPY_SEARCH = [{"url":"wiki/index.html","title":"The canopy wiki","section":"Wiki","lede":"Learn git with canopy: one guide for canopy desktop, one for canopy console, and the ideas both share.","headings":[{"id":"which","text":"Which app is for me?"},{"id":"organised","text":"How the wiki is organised"},{"id":"both","text":"For both apps"},{"id":"desktop","text":"canopy desktop"},{"id":"console","text":"canopy console"}],"text":"canopy is a friendlier way to use git. It comes as two apps that work on the same repositories and share the same settings and themes: canopy desktop , a window you click around in, and canopy console , a keyboard-driven program that runs in your terminal. This wiki has a full guide for each, plus the ideas they share. New to git? Read Git in ten minutes first. It takes a few minutes, and everything else makes more sense afterwards. Then pick your app below. Which app is for me? # canopy desktop is the one to start with. It runs on macOS, Windows and Linux, shows what git is doing in plain words, and asks before anything risky. Everything is a click or a shortcut away, and it can show you the git command behind each action so you learn as you go. Its guide starts with the canopy desktop guide . canopy console is the power tool. It lives in your terminal, works over SSH, and is built for speed: one key per action, and screens for things canopy desktop leaves out, such as interactive rebase, bisect, worktrees, the reflog and issues. If you already live in a terminal, start with the canopy console guide . You don't have to choose. Both open the same repository, so you can commit in one and push in the other, and switching between them costs nothing. How the wiki is organised # Each guide has the same shape, so what you learn in one carries over to the other: Start here: a tour of every page or screen. Guides: the daily loop, commits, branches, syncing with a remote, and working with GitHub. Recipes: short, step-by-step fixes for everyday jams, such as undoing a mistake or resolving a conflict. Reference: shortcuts, settings, troubleshooting and the FAQ. Two pages are shared by both apps: Git in ten minutes , which explains the ideas behind git, and Themes , which covers the built-in themes and making your own. The guides are listed below. Every page has the wiki's sidebar on the left, and the search box at the top of the site finds any page. For both apps # Git in ten minutes Repositories, the staging area, commits, branches and remotes, each shown where it lives in canopy. Themes The built-in themes, and making your own. canopy desktop # The friendly app for macOS, Windows and Linux. Start here if you're new to git. The canopy desktop guide The friendly app for macOS, Windows and Linux. Start here if you're new to git. A tour of canopy desktop Home, Changes, History, Branches, Stash and the GitHub pages, one at a time. canopy console # The keyboard-driven power tool that runs in your terminal, with everything canopy can do. The canopy console guide The keyboard-driven power tool that runs in your terminal, with everything canopy can do. A tour of canopy console Home, Changes, Log, Refs, Stash, Repos, Reflog, PRs, Issues and CI, one at a time."},{"url":"wiki/git-basics.html","title":"Git in ten minutes","section":"Wiki","lede":"The handful of ideas that make git make sense, and where to see each one in canopy.","headings":[{"id":"repo","text":"A repository is a project with a memory"},{"id":"three-places","text":"Your changes live in three places"},{"id":"commits","text":"A commit is a saved snapshot"},{"id":"branches","text":"A branch is a line of work"},{"id":"remotes","text":"A remote is the same repo somewhere else"},{"id":"safety","text":"You can almost always undo"},{"id":"related","text":"Where to next"}],"text":"These few ideas are all of git you need to get going. They're the same in canopy desktop and canopy console: each section says where to see the idea in both apps, and links to the full guide for each. A repository is a project with a memory # A repository (repo) is a folder whose history git tracks. Everything git knows lives in a hidden .git folder inside it. Open a repo in canopy desktop, or run canopy in it, and Home shows where things stand ( ⌘1 in canopy desktop, 1 in canopy console). canopy console: Home Your changes live in three places # The working tree is the files you edit. Changes here are listed under Changes on the Changes page ( ⌘2 in canopy desktop, 2 in canopy console). The staging area (also called the index) is where you gather what goes into the next commit. Staged files appear under Staged . History is the commits themselves, on the History page ( ⌘3 in canopy desktop, 3 in canopy console). Staging is what lets you commit part of your work. In canopy desktop you can stage a whole file with its + button, or click lines in its diff and press Stage N lines . In canopy console, stage a file with space , or step into its diff with ↵ and stage single lines. See Write good commits (desktop) or the same page for console . canopy console: Changes A commit is a saved snapshot # A commit records the staged changes with a message saying why. Each commit points to the one before it, which is how history forms a chain. In canopy desktop, write a summary in the commit box and press Commit . In canopy console, press c , write a short summary (a counter runs up to 50 characters), and press ↵ . Good messages say why : \"Fix crash when the repo has no commits\" beats \"update main.rs\". A branch is a line of work # A branch is a movable name for a commit. When you commit, your current branch moves forward. Branches let you try something without disturbing main . The Branches page lists them: New branch… and Check out in canopy desktop, 4 then n and space in canopy console. See Branches, merging and rebasing (desktop) or the console version . HEAD means \"where you are now\"; History shows it as HEAD → main . A remote is the same repo somewhere else # A remote (usually called origin ) is a copy of the repo on a server such as GitHub. Push sends your commits there; pull brings others' commits to you; fetch checks what's new without changing your files. Each is a button in canopy desktop's top bar, and P , p and f in canopy console. canopy shows ↑2 ↓1 when you're 2 commits ahead and 1 behind. See Sync with a remote (desktop) or the console version . You can almost always undo # Git rarely forgets anything. Undo takes back your last commit, reset, merge or checkout: ⌘Z (Ctrl+Z on Windows and Linux) in canopy desktop, z in canopy console. canopy console's Reflog tab ( 7 ) lists everywhere you've been so you can go back further. See Undo a mistake (desktop) or the console version . Learn as you go: canopy console's teach mode is on by default and shows the git command behind every action in the footer. canopy desktop does the same with Show git commands in Settings. The ideas on this page turn into commands you'll recognize. Where to next # canopy desktop: Your daily workflow , Write good commits , Branches , Sync with a remote , Undo a mistake canopy console: Your daily workflow , Write good commits , Branches , Sync with a remote , Undo a mistake Both: Themes"},{"url":"wiki/themes.html","title":"Themes","section":"Wiki","lede":"The built-in themes, how to switch in each app, and how to make your own.","headings":[{"id":"switching","text":"Switching theme"},{"id":"switch-desktop","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"switch-console","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"built-in","text":"The built-in themes"},{"id":"theme-canopy","text":"canopy"},{"id":"theme-catppuccin","text":"catppuccin"},{"id":"theme-gruvbox","text":"gruvbox"},{"id":"theme-nord","text":"nord"},{"id":"theme-light","text":"light"},{"id":"your-own","text":"Your own themes"},{"id":"desktop","text":"With the theme editor in canopy desktop"},{"id":"sync","text":"Moving settings between the apps"}],"text":"canopy desktop and canopy console share their themes: the same built-in five, and the same folder for your own. Pick one in either app, or make one and use it in both. Switching theme # In canopy desktop # Open Settings (the gear at the bottom of the sidebar, ⌘, or Ctrl+, on Windows and Linux), choose Appearance , and click a theme's card. Auto follows your computer's light or dark setting. The change applies at once and is saved. In canopy console # Press ⌃T to cycle through the themes (built-in and your own). To make one stick, set theme = \"nord\" in config.toml , or start with canopy --theme nord for a single run. canopy --list-themes lists everything canopy found. The built-in themes # Five come with canopy: canopy (dark), catppuccin, gruvbox, nord and light. The samples below use the colors canopy console draws with; canopy desktop uses the same colors. canopy # ● main f5be172 + added line - removed line 3h ago background #101612 text #d6e2d6 accent #7ac878 highlight #e6be6e added #7ac878 removed #e86e64 modified #e6be6e commit #a096e6 branch #6ec8c8 remote #78a0e6 catppuccin # ● main f5be172 + added line - removed line 3h ago background #1e1e2e text #cdd6f4 accent #cba6f7 highlight #89b4fa added #a6e3a1 removed #f38ba8 modified #f9e2af commit #b4befe branch #94e2d5 remote #89b4fa gruvbox # ● main f5be172 + added line - removed line 3h ago background #282828 text #ebdbb2 accent #fabd2f highlight #83a598 added #b8bb26 removed #fb4934 modified #fabd2f commit #d3869b branch #8ec07c remote #83a598 nord # ● main f5be172 + added line - removed line 3h ago background #2e3440 text #d8dee9 accent #88c0d0 highlight #b48ead added #a3be8c removed #bf616a modified #ebcb8b commit #b48ead branch #8fbcbb remote #81a1c1 light # ● main f5be172 + added line - removed line 3h ago background #fafaf6 text #282c28 accent #2e8c46 highlight #b06e14 added #288c3c removed #c83232 modified #b06e14 commit #6450be branch #14828c remote #285ab4 Your own themes # A theme is a small JSON file in ~/.canopy/themes/ (on Windows, %USERPROFILE%\\.canopy\\themes ). Both apps read that folder, so a theme made in one shows up in the other. Start from a built-in one: mkdir -p ~/.canopy/themes canopy --export-theme nord > ~/.canopy/themes/ocean.json Then change its name and any colors you like. Colors you leave out come from base : { \"name\": \"Ocean\", \"base\": \"nord\", \"colors\": { \"bg\": \"#0f1b2a\", \"accent\": \"#4fc1e9\", \"selection_bg\": \"#1d3350\" } } Use it with theme = \"Ocean\" in the config or canopy -t Ocean ; ⌃T cycles through your themes too. A file with a mistake in it is skipped with a note. Color Used for bg , fg , muted Background, text, and quieter text (times, hints) border , border_focus Panel borders, and the focused panel's border accent , accent_alt Titles and highlights, and a second highlight selection_bg The selected row added , removed , modified , conflict File states and diff lines added_bg , removed_bg Behind added and removed diff lines hash , branch , remote , tag Commit ids, branches, remotes, tags warn , error Warnings and errors With the theme editor in canopy desktop # In Settings → Appearance, Customize… opens an editor: a name, and a color picker plus hex box for each color. The app changes as you pick, and Save theme stores only the colors that differ from the theme you started from. Themes you made appear under Your themes , with Edit… , Edit JSON , Export… , Import… and Delete . Saved themes go in the same folder, so canopy console can use them straight away. See the desktop Settings page . Moving settings between the apps # canopy console is set up in config.toml ; canopy desktop has its own Settings page. They can swap the settings they share (theme, text editor, AI assistant, showing git commands) as one JSON file: canopy --export-settings > my-canopy.json # theme, editor, ai, teach_mode canopy --import-settings my-canopy.json # updates config.toml (and saves the theme) In canopy desktop, Settings → General has Import from canopy console "},{"url":"wiki/desktop/index.html","title":"The canopy desktop guide","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Everything in canopy desktop, page by page: guides for the daily loop, recipes for everyday fixes, and reference for shortcuts and settings.","headings":[{"id":"organised","text":"How this guide is organised"},{"id":"start-here","text":"Start here"},{"id":"guides","text":"Guides"},{"id":"recipes","text":"Recipes"},{"id":"reference","text":"Reference"}],"text":"canopy desktop is the friendly way to use git: a window with your repository's state on Home, a page for changes, history, branches and stashes, and pages for GitHub pull requests, issues, CI runs and notifications. This guide is for anyone using it, and needs no git background; where a word is new, the page explains it. If you have never used git, read Git in ten minutes first, then the tour . If you already know git, skim the tour and go straight to the recipes. How this guide is organised # Start here walks through every page of the app. The Guides explain the everyday jobs (the daily loop, commits, branches, syncing and GitHub). The Recipes each fix one specific problem, from a typo in a commit message to a failing CI run. Reference lists shortcuts and settings, and answers common questions. Each guide and recipe shows the buttons to click, then the plain git commands canopy runs for you, so you learn git as you use it. Prefer the keyboard and a terminal? The canopy console guide covers the same jobs there. Start here # A tour of canopy desktop Home, Changes, History, Branches, Stash and the GitHub pages, one at a time. Guides # Your daily workflow Pull, branch, edit, stage, commit, push, open a PR: the loop you'll do every day. Write good commits Small commits, clear messages, and staging just the lines that belong together. Branches, merging and rebasing Why branches, how merging and rebasing differ, and what to do when they collide. Sync with a remote Fetch, pull and push; ahead and behind; a diverged branch; force-with-lease. Working with GitHub Connect GitHub, then pull requests, CI and notifications. Recipes # Fix your last commit Typo in the message, a forgotten file, or a fix for an older commit. Undo a mistake Undo a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back \"lost\" work. Resolve a merge conflict Pick a side (or both) for each conflict, then finish the merge or rebase. Put work aside with the stash Save half-done work, switch tasks, and bring it back later. Ignore files with .gitignore Keep build output, secrets and clutter out of git. Tag a version and publish a release Tag a commit, push the tag, and publish a GitHub release. Review a pull request Read the diff, comment, then approve or request changes. Fix a failing CI run Find why CI failed, fix it, push, and re-run. Start a new repository Set up a folder, then connect GitHub over HTTPS or SSH. Reference # Keyboard shortcuts Every shortcut in canopy desktop. Settings Themes, refresh, pull mode, updates, and the AI assistant. Syntax highlighting and live refresh Colored diffs and instant updates. Troubleshooting Fixes for the problems people actually hit. FAQ Short answers to common questions."},{"url":"wiki/desktop/tour.html","title":"A tour of canopy desktop","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Every page of the desktop app, what it shows, and what you can do there.","headings":[{"id":"welcome","text":"The welcome screen"},{"id":"layout","text":"The window"},{"id":"home","text":"Home"},{"id":"new-to-git","text":"The \"New to git?\" card"},{"id":"changes","text":"Changes"},{"id":"history","text":"History"},{"id":"branches","text":"Branches"},{"id":"stash","text":"Stash"},{"id":"github","text":"The GitHub pages"},{"id":"prs","text":"Pull requests"},{"id":"issues","text":"Issues"},{"id":"actions","text":"Actions"},{"id":"notifications","text":"Notifications"},{"id":"undo","text":"Undo"},{"id":"settings","text":"Settings"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"canopy desktop is a window with a sidebar on the left and one page on the right. The sidebar lists the pages you use on the open repository, and the toolbar above every page has the buttons you use most: Fetch , Pull , Push , undo and refresh. This page walks through the app in the order you meet it: the welcome screen, the window, then each sidebar page. Everything canopy does is a normal git command. With Show git commands on (Settings, General), a notice shows the command it ran after each action. The welcome screen # Until a repository is open, canopy shows the welcome screen. The welcome screen, with the repositories you opened recently. Open a repository… ( ⌘O (Ctrl+O on Windows and Linux)) picks a folder that is already a git repository. Start a new one… turns a folder into a repository and can put it on GitHub. See Start a new repository . Recent lists the repositories you opened before. Click one to open it. The small x on a row removes it from the list, and Settings opens the settings. The window # Repository switcher (top of the sidebar): shows the repository name and the branch you are on. Click it to close this repository and return to the welcome screen. Sidebar pages : Home, Changes, History, Branches and Stash, then a GitHub group with Pull requests, Issues, Actions and Notifications. A number beside a page is a count: changed files or conflicts, branches (shown when you have more than one), stashes, and unread notifications. Toolbar : the page title, then pills for commits to push ( ↑N ) or pull ( ↓N ), the Fetch , Pull and Push buttons, an undo button and a refresh button. Bottom of the sidebar : your GitHub account (click it to see your account, your git name and email, and whether git and the GitHub CLI are found), the gear for Settings, and the canopy version. The first nine pages have shortcuts: ⌘1 to ⌘9 (Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+9 on Windows and Linux) go to them in sidebar order, so ⌘1 is Home and ⌘9 is Notifications. ⌘R refreshes by hand, though canopy also notices changes on its own. See keyboard shortcuts for the full list. Home # The starting point. It shows where you are and what to do next. Home: the repository, its counts, and the next thing to do. A card with the repository name, its folder, the branch you are on ( on main ), the branch it follows ( tracks origin/main ) and how far ahead or behind you are. During a merge or rebase it also shows a badge saying so. Tiles for Staged , Changed , Conflicts , Stashes , Branches and Remotes . Each has a small ? that explains the word in plain language. Next steps : plain sentences such as \"1 changed file(s). Stage what you want, then commit.\" with a button that does it, such as Open Changes , Commit , Push , Pull , Open History or Open Branches . A GitHub card when the repository is on GitHub and you are set up: the pull request for your branch, reviews waiting for you and unread notifications. If the latest CI run failed, a banner offers Details , View in GitHub and Fix with your AI assistant. Recent commits (the latest 12) and Branches (up to 10). The \"New to git?\" card # The first time, Home also shows a short card, \"New to git? Four ideas\": change, stage, commit and push. Dismiss it when you are done. Show it again in Settings, General brings it back. The intro card, and the explanation that opens from a tile's question mark. Changes # Where you stage and commit. Files are listed on the left in groups, and the diff of the selected file is on the right. The groups are Conflicts (only during a merge or rebase), Staged and Changes . A letter beside each file says what happened to it: M changed, A added, D deleted, R renamed. Changes: pick lines in the diff, stage them, and commit. Click the + beside a file to stage it, or &minus; to unstage it. Stage all and Unstage all do every file. With the file list focused, ↑ and ↓ (or j and k ) move the selection and space stages or unstages the selected file. Above the diff: Open in editor , Stage file (or Unstage file )"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/daily-workflow.html","title":"Your daily workflow","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"The loop you'll repeat every day in canopy desktop: pull, branch, edit, stage, commit, push, open a pull request.","headings":[{"id":"loop","text":"The loop, in one line each"},{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"tips","text":"Tips"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Most days with git follow one loop: get up to date, make a branch, change things, commit in small pieces, push, and open a pull request. This page walks through it once in canopy desktop, and links to the page that explains each step. The loop assumes your repository is on GitHub. If it is not, skip the pull request steps; everything else still applies. The loop, in one line each # Pull so you start from the latest work. Branch for the change, named after it. Edit your files in your editor. Stage and commit , in small, focused commits. Push the branch. Open a pull request , and watch CI. In canopy desktop # Start up to date. Open your repository ( ⌘O (Ctrl+O on Windows and Linux), or pick it from the Recent list) and press Pull in the top bar. Home shows ↑N and ↓N pills for how many commits you are ahead of or behind the remote. Fetch updates those numbers without changing your files. Make a branch. On Branches , press New branch… , name it after the change, like fix-login-timeout , and press Create branch . With Switch to it ticked (the default), canopy moves onto it. See Branches, merging and rebasing . Edit your files in your editor as usual. canopy notices changes on its own (Settings, General, Refresh ); ⌘R (Ctrl+R on Windows and Linux) refreshes by hand. Review and stage. On Changes , read each diff. Click + to stage a file, or pick lines and press Stage N lines to stage only some. Commit. Write a Summary and press Commit N files , or ⌘↵ (Ctrl+Enter on Windows and Linux). Repeat steps 3 to 5 as you go. See Write good commits . Push. Press Push . The first push of a new branch also sets it to follow the remote, so later pushes and pulls need nothing more. If a dialog says the branch has diverged, see Sync with a remote . Open a pull request. On Pull requests , press New PR… . The title is filled in from your last commit; add a description of what changed and why, and press Create pull request . Tick Open as a draft if it is not ready for review. Watch CI. Actions lists the runs for your branch, and Home's GitHub card shows whether checks pass. See Working with GitHub . Home is the place to start each day: where you are, what changed, and what to do next. Home's Next steps card suggests the next move from wherever you are, with a button that does it: Open Changes when files have changed, Push when you have commits to send, Pull when you are behind. What git runs # $ git pull $ git switch -c fix-login-timeout $ git add -- src/login.rs $ git commit -m \"Raise the login timeout to 30 seconds\" $ git push --set-upstream origin fix-login-timeout $ gh pr create --title \"Raise the login timeout\" --body \"...\" canopy adds --progress to fetch, pull and push so it can show a progress bar. The pull request is made by the GitHub CLI, gh , which canopy uses for everything on GitHub. Turn on Show git commands in Settings to see each command as it runs. Tips # Need to switch tasks but not ready to commit? Put the work aside with the stash. See Put work aside with the stash . Committed too early? Amend the last commit. See Fix your last commit . Commit often and small. Small commits are easier to review, and easier to undo. If something goes wrong # Pull stops with a conflict. Both sides changed the same lines. Follow Resolve a merge conflict . You worked on main by mistake. Make a branch from where you are before you push; your commits come with you. Then move main back; see Undo a mistake . You committed the wrong thing. Press the undo button in the top bar, or ⌘Z (Ctrl+Z on Windows and Linux). Your changes come back as staged files. Push says \"Nothing to push: pull first.\" The remote has moved and you have nothing new. Press Pull . If you have new commits too, canopy opens the diverged dialog instead; see Sync with a remote . New PR says \"Push <branch> first, then open a pull request for it.\" A pull request needs the branch on GitHub. Press Push , then try again. Related pages # Git in ten minutes Write good commits Branches, merging and "},{"url":"wiki/desktop/commits.html","title":"Write good commits","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Small, focused commits with clear messages, and how to stage exactly the lines that belong together.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"message","text":"Write a good message"},{"id":"stage","text":"Choose what goes in"},{"id":"commit","text":"Make the commit"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A commit saves a snapshot of what you have staged , together with a message that says why. This page covers writing the message, choosing exactly what goes into a commit, and where to fix one you have just made. If the words working tree, staging area and commit are new, read Git in ten minutes first. In canopy desktop # Write a good message # Summary: one short line, about 50 characters. Say what the change does: \"Ignore case when counting words\". Description (optional): why you did it, or what a reader might miss. canopy puts a blank line between the two for you. Say why , not just what . \"Fix crash on an empty file\" beats \"update main.rs\". Only commit related changes together. If a summary needs the word \"and\", you probably have two commits. Staging single lines or blocks, below, is how you split them. Choose what goes in # Open Changes ( ⌘2 (Ctrl+2 on Windows and Linux)). Changed files are listed under Changes . Files already staged are under Staged . To stage a whole file, click the + beside it, or select it and press space . Stage all stages everything. Unstage all , or the − beside a file, takes things back out. To stage only part of a file, select it and look at the diff. Click a + or − line to pick it (shift-click picks a range), then press Stage N lines . Or press Stage hunk above a block of changes to stage the whole block. For a staged file, Unstage N lines and Unstage hunk take pieces back out. The file's own buttons are Stage file (or Unstage file ), Discard… and Open in editor . Discard… throws away your unstaged edits to that file for good, so it asks first. Three lines picked; only those go into the commit. The commit box is at the bottom left. Make the commit # Type a Summary (required) in the commit box, and a Description if it helps. Press Commit N files , or ⌘↵ (Ctrl+Enter on Windows and Linux). If nothing is staged, the button reads Stage all & commit and stages every change first. To change the commit you just made instead, tick Amend last commit ; the button becomes Amend . See Fix your last commit . An empty summary shows \"Write a short summary of what changed first.\" and nothing is committed. canopy desktop does not count characters, so keep the summary short yourself. With Show git commands on in Settings, each action shows the git command it ran. What git runs # Staging and committing are a few plain commands. Staging lines or a block builds a small patch and applies it to the staging area only, so your files are not touched. $ git add -- src/main.rs $ git add --all $ git restore --staged -- src/main.rs $ git apply --cached --unidiff-zero --whitespace=nowarn - $ git commit -m \"Ignore case when counting words\" $ git commit -m \"Ignore case when counting words\" --amend Un-staging lines uses the same git apply --cached with --reverse . A description is added to the message after a blank line. If something goes wrong # The commit box says \"Nothing to commit\". There are no changes, or you have not saved your files. Save in your editor and try again. You committed too much or too little. If you have not pushed, amend it ( Fix your last commit ) or undo it with ⌘Z (Ctrl+Z on Windows and Linux). Your changes stay in your files, staged. See Undo a mistake . You committed a file that should be ignored. See Ignore files with .gitignore . Git says it does not know who you are. Set user.name and user.email with git config --global in a terminal, then commit again. The commit is already pushed. Do not rewrite it. Make a new commit that fixes it, or see Undo a mistake . Related pages # Git in ten minutes Fix your last commit Undo a mistake Ignore files with .gitignore Sync with a remote"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/branches.html","title":"Branches, merging and rebasing","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"What branches are for, the two ways to combine them, and what to do when they collide.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"merge-rebase","text":"Merge or rebase?"},{"id":"do-it","text":"Work with branches"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A branch is a movable name for a commit. When you commit, the branch you are on moves forward. That is all it is, which is why branches are cheap: make one for every change, and main stays working while you experiment. This page covers making, switching, renaming and deleting branches, bringing two branches together with a merge or a rebase, and what to do when they collide. In canopy desktop # Merge or rebase? # Both bring one branch's commits into another. They differ in the history they leave. Merge Rebase What it does Joins the two lines with a merge commit that has two parents. Replays your commits on top of the other branch, as if you had started from its latest commit. History Keeps what really happened, including the fork. A straight line, no merge commit. Your commits Unchanged. Rewritten: they get new hashes. Best for Bringing a finished branch into main , or any commits others already have. Tidying your own unshared branch before you open a pull request. The one rule. Never rebase commits other people have already pulled. Rebasing rewrites them, and their copies will no longer match yours. Work with branches # Open Branches ( ⌘4 (Ctrl+4 on Windows and Linux)). Its tabs are Local , Remote , Tags and Remotes , each with a count. A local branch shows ↑N or ↓N when it is ahead of or behind its upstream. The Local tab. New branch… is at the top right. Create. Press New branch… , type a name such as feature/my-idea , and press Create branch . It starts from where you are now. Leave Switch to it ticked to move onto it straight away. Switch. Press Check out on a branch. If you have changes that the switch would overwrite, git refuses and says so; commit or stash them first (see Put work aside with the stash ). Merge. Check out the branch that should receive the changes, then press Merge into <branch> on the other one and confirm Merge . Rebase. Check out your own branch, then press Rebase onto on the branch you want to build on and confirm Rebase . The dialog reminds you not to rebase commits others already pulled. Rename. Press Rename , edit the name and press Rename . The branch you are on shows only Rename . Delete. Press Delete . The dialog offers Delete and Force delete . If the branch has commits that are not merged anywhere, plain delete is refused; force delete removes it anyway, and those commits can then only be found through the reflog. Remote branches. On the Remote tab, Check out makes a local branch that tracks it, and Delete on remote removes it from the server for everyone, after a confirm. A branch's upstream is set by its first Push ; there is no separate button for it. What git runs # $ git switch -c feature/my-idea $ git switch main $ git merge --no-edit feature/my-idea $ git rebase main $ git branch -m old-name new-name $ git branch -d feature/my-idea $ git branch -D feature/my-idea $ git push origin --delete feature/my-idea With Switch to it unticked, canopy runs git branch feature/my-idea instead of git switch -c . Check out on a remote branch runs git switch --track origin/feature/my-idea . -d refuses to delete unmerged work; -D does not. If something goes wrong # The merge or rebase stops and a banner says there is a conflict. Both branches changed the same lines. Follow Resolve a merge conflict , or press Abort on the banner to go back to where you were. Git refuses to switch branches. You have changes it would overwrite. Commit them, or stash them ( Put work aside with the stash ), and switch again. Git refuses to delete a branch. It has commits that are not merged anywhere. Merge it first, or use Force delete only if you are sure you do not need them. You deleted a branch you needed. Its commits stay in the reflog for a while. See Undo a mistake . You merged or rebased the wrong way. Undo it with ⌘Z (Ctrl+Z on Windows and Linux). After a rebase, push is refused or opens a dialog. Your commits were rewritten, so the branch has diverged from the remote. See Sync with a remote . Related pages # Reso"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/syncing.html","title":"Sync with a remote","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Fetch, pull and push, what \"ahead\" and \"behind\" mean, and what to do when your branch and the remote have both moved on.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"actions","text":"Fetch, pull and push"},{"id":"pull-mode","text":"The Pull setting"},{"id":"diverged","text":"When your branch has diverged"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Your repository lives on your computer. A remote (usually called origin ) is another copy, for example on GitHub. Three actions move commits between the two: fetch looks, pull brings other people's commits to you, and push sends yours. The pills in the top bar show where you stand. ↑1 means one commit only you have (ahead). ↓2 means two commits only the remote has (behind). No pills means neither. In canopy desktop # Fetch, pull and push # Button What it does Changes your files? Fetch Downloads what is new on every remote, so the ahead and behind counts are up to date. No. Pull Fetches, then brings the remote's new commits into your current branch. Yes: new commits are added, and a merge or rebase may happen. Push Sends your new commits to the remote. No. It changes the remote. Fetch is always safe, so use it whenever you like. Pull before you push if the remote may have moved. Use Fetch , Pull and Push in the top bar. A progress bar shows git's own progress lines while they run. Press Push to send your commits. If the branch has no upstream yet, canopy pushes to the remote of its upstream if it has one, else origin , else your first remote, and sets the upstream for you. If you are behind and not ahead, Push says \"Nothing to push: pull first.\" Press Pull instead. If there is nothing to send and nothing to bring in, it says \"Nothing to push: already up to date.\" Press Pull on a branch that was never pushed and canopy says it \"isn't on the remote yet, so there's nothing to pull.\" Push it first. After a pull or push, the pills update. Undo a pull with ⌘Z (Ctrl+Z on Windows and Linux). The Pull setting # In Settings ( ⌘, (Ctrl+, on Windows and Linux)) under General , the Pull setting decides what Pull does when both sides have new commits. Default follows your git config ( pull.rebase ), Merge always merges, Rebase always rebases. When your branch has diverged # A branch has diverged when you and the remote both have commits the other lacks, for example 1 ahead and 2 behind. Git will not simply push, because that would overwrite the remote's commits. Pressing Push opens \"Your branch has diverged\" with these choices: Button What happens Use it when Pull with merge Creates a merge commit joining the two lines. You want to keep history exactly as it happened. Pull with rebase (highlighted) Replays your commits on top of the remote's. Then press Push again. Your commits get new hashes. You want a straight line of history. Force push Replaces the remote branch with yours. The commits only on the remote are lost from that branch. You rewrote your own commits on purpose and nobody else has built on them. Cancel Nothing happens. You want to look first. Pushing a diverged branch. Pulling is the safe way out; Force push is red for a reason. Force push is the dangerous one. canopy asks once more, with the number of commits that will be lost, and pushes with --force-with-lease . That refuses if someone pushed since your last fetch, which protects their newer work. It still discards whatever is on the remote branch that you have not merged. When unsure, choose a pull. Both pulls can stop on a conflict; see Resolve a merge conflict . The diverged dialog only appears when you press Push . If you press Pull on a diverged branch, git uses your Pull setting, or its own config. What git runs # canopy adds --progress to fetch, pull and push so it can show progress; it is left out below except where it always appears. $ git fetch --prune --all $ git pull $ git pull --rebase $ git pull --no-rebase $ git push origin main $ git push --set-upstream origin main $ git push --force-with-lease origin main Pull with the Default setting runs plain git pull . Choosing Merge or Rebase in Settings adds --no-rebase or --rebase , and so do the two pull buttons in the diverged dialog. Fetch also prunes remote branches that were deleted on the server. --force-with-lease overwrites the remote only if it is still where you last saw it. If something goes wrong # \"This b"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/github-flow.html","title":"Working with GitHub","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Connect canopy desktop to GitHub, then handle pull requests, CI runs and notifications without opening a browser.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"connect","text":"Connect GitHub"},{"id":"pages","text":"The GitHub pages"},{"id":"prs","text":"Pull requests"},{"id":"issues","text":"Issues"},{"id":"actions","text":"Actions"},{"id":"notifications","text":"Notifications"},{"id":"home-card","text":"The Home GitHub card"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"canopy desktop handles pull requests, issues, CI runs and notifications without a browser tab. It does this through the official GitHub CLI, gh , using your own login: canopy has no separate account and stores no token. You connect once. After that the GitHub pages work, and Home shows a GitHub card for the branch you are on. In canopy desktop # Connect GitHub # canopy never installs anything for you. If gh is missing or you are logged out, each GitHub page shows a card that says exactly what to run. To do it yourself: $ brew install gh $ gh auth login On Windows use winget install --id GitHub.cli -e , and on Linux see cli.github.com. Follow the prompts of gh auth login in a terminal, then come back and press Try again on the card. The card says What it means Connect GitHub gh is not installed. Install it, then log in. Log in to GitHub gh is installed but not logged in. Run gh auth login . Not a GitHub repository This repository has no GitHub remote, so there is nothing to show. See Start a new repository to add one. Everything on the git pages still works. The GitHub pages # The sidebar's GitHub group holds Pull requests , Issues , Actions and Notifications , reached with ⌘6 to ⌘9 (Ctrl+6 to Ctrl+9 on Windows and Linux). Pull requests # The pull request list on the left, one pull request open on the right. Find one. Filter by Open , Mine , To review or All . Select a pull request to see its description, checks, reviews and comments. Create one. Push your branch first, then press New PR… . The title is filled in from your last commit; add a description, optionally set Into to another base branch, and tick Open as a draft if it is not ready. Press Create pull request . If the branch has not been pushed, canopy says \"Push <branch> first, then open a pull request for it.\" Work on one. The buttons are View in GitHub , Check out (to run it locally), Comment… , Review… , Close… and Merge… . A banner warns when the pull request conflicts with its base branch. Review. Review… offers Approve, Request changes or Comment only. A comment is required unless you approve. Press Submit review . Merge. Merge… asks how: Squash and merge (one commit) (the default), Create a merge commit or Rebase and merge . Delete <branch> afterwards is ticked by default. Press Merge . Issues # Filter by Open , Assigned to me or All . New issue… takes a title and a description. An issue can be commented on, closed and reopened. Actions # The tabs are This branch and All branches . Select a run to see its jobs. For a failed run, Details shows the log and Re-run failed jobs tries again. Fix with your AI assistant hands it the failure; set an assistant up in Settings. See Fix a failing CI run . Notifications # Notifications from every repository, or only this one. Switch between All repositories and This repository . Tick Read too to include ones you have read. Press Mark all read to clear the list; canopy asks first. Select a notification for its buttons: Open in canopy , View in GitHub and Mark as read . The Home GitHub card # On Home , the GitHub card shows the pull request for your current branch (its number, title, review state and how many checks passed), how many pull requests are waiting for your review, and how many notifications are unread. Click a count to jump to that list. If your latest CI run failed, a red banner offers Details , View in GitHub and Fix with your assistant. What git runs # These are gh commands rather than git. canopy runs them for you, and shows them when Show git commands is on in Settings. $ gh auth status $ gh pr list $ gh pr checkout 12 $ gh pr create --title \"Add top_words\" --body \"...\" --base main --draft $ gh pr review 12 --approve $ gh pr merge 12 --squash --delete-branch $ gh issue create --title \"Crash on an empty file\" --body \"...\" $ gh run list $ gh run rerun 101 --failed --base and --draft are added only when you fill in Into or tick Open as a draft . --delete-branch is added only when Delete <branch> afterwards is tick"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/fix-last-commit.html","title":"Fix your last commit","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"A typo in the message, a forgotten file, or a fix that belongs in an older commit.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"amend","text":"Fix the message or add a forgotten file"},{"id":"older","text":"A change that belongs in an older commit"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Everyone makes a small mistake in a commit: a typo in the message, a file you forgot to add, or a change that belongs in a commit from a few steps back. Git can fix all three, and canopy desktop keeps the first two to a few clicks. Fixing a commit really replaces it with a new one (it gets a new id). That is harmless while the commit is only on your computer. If you have already pushed it, read If something goes wrong first. In canopy desktop # Fix the message or add a forgotten file # Both jobs use the Amend last commit box under the file list on the Changes page. Open Changes in the sidebar. To add a forgotten file or a small edit, select it and click Stage file (or press space while the file list has focus, or click Stage all ). To fix only the message, stage nothing. Tick Amend last commit . If the summary box is empty, canopy fills the summary and description boxes with the last commit's message. Change the words you want. Click Amend ( ⌘↵ , or Ctrl+↵ on Windows and Linux). The last commit is replaced by one with your new message and the staged files included. A notice says Amended the last commit . Amend last commit fills in the old message, and the button changes to Amend. Only staged files are added. Amend never stages anything for you. Files you have not staged stay out of the commit, so stage the forgotten file first. (A normal commit with nothing staged does stage everything; amend does not.) A change that belongs in an older commit # canopy desktop can't edit a commit that is older than the last one, because that needs an interactive rebase, which canopy console has (see Fix your last commit in canopy console ). In canopy desktop you have two choices: The simple way: make the fix and commit it as a new commit with a clear message such as Fix typo from the parser change . Nothing is rewritten, so it is safe even after you have pushed. If nothing has been pushed: open History , select the commit just before the one you want to fix, and click Reset to here… , then Soft . Every change after that commit comes back as staged changes. Make your fix and commit again. This turns all the later commits into one, so use it only when that is fine. Soft keeps every undone change staged, ready to commit again. What git runs # These are the commands canopy desktop runs for the fixes above: $ git add -- <file> $ git commit -m \"the new message\" --amend $ git reset --soft <commit> --amend replaces the last commit instead of adding a new one. With Show git commands turned on in Settings, each notice also shows the command it ran. If something goes wrong # You amended and it was the wrong thing. Press ⌘Z (Ctrl+Z on Windows and Linux) or click the undo arrow in the top bar. The branch goes back to the commit as it was before, with your changes staged. See Undo a mistake . \"Write a short summary of what changed first.\" The summary box is empty. Type a summary, then click again. You already pushed the commit. An amended commit is a different commit, so the branch now shows as diverged and Push asks what to do. Force push replaces the remote copy with yours (it uses --force-with-lease , so it stops if someone else pushed in the meantime). Only do that on a branch nobody else is using; otherwise leave the old commit alone and add a new commit with the fix. See Sync with a remote . Amend picked up more than you wanted. Undo, unstage what you did not mean to include (the − button on the file, or Unstage all ), and amend again. A rebase or merge is in progress. Finish or abort it first. See Resolve a merge conflict . Related pages # Undo a mistake Write good commits Sync with a remote Fix your last commit in canopy console (can also fix older commits)"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/undo.html","title":"Undo a mistake","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Take back a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back work you thought was lost.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"reset","text":"Go back further: reset"},{"id":"revert","text":"Undo a commit you already shared: revert"},{"id":"discard","text":"Throw away uncommitted edits on purpose"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Git rarely loses anything. A commit, a merge, a reset or a checkout can nearly always be taken back, because git remembers where your branch used to point. The one thing it cannot bring back is edits you never committed and then threw away. The quick way is the undo arrow in the top bar. It looks at the last thing that moved your branch, tells you exactly what it will do, and only does it when you agree. For anything older, use a reset. In canopy desktop # Click the undo arrow in the top bar, next to the refresh button (or press ⌘Z , Ctrl+Z on Windows and Linux, while you are not typing in a text box). A dialog, Undo the last action? , shows the last action and what will happen. Click Undo to go ahead or Cancel to leave everything alone. The dialog says exactly what Undo will do before it does it. After a commit, a merge or a similar action, the notice at the bottom also reminds you: Undo with ⌘Z if that wasn't what you meant. What Undo does depends on what you did last: Last action Undo does this A commit (or an amend) Moves the branch back one step. The commit's changes come back as staged changes, ready to fix and commit again. A checkout (switching branch) Switches back to the branch you came from. Anything else that moved the branch, such as a merge, pull, cherry-pick, revert, reset or rebase Moves the branch back to where it was before, and keeps your uncommitted changes. A whole rebase counts as one action. Undo is built from git's reflog (its private diary of where your branch has been), so it can't take back a push, a discard or a dropped stash. If it has nothing to go back to, you see Nothing to undo yet. Go back further: reset # Open History in the sidebar and click the commit you want to go back to. Click Reset to here… in the details on the right. The dialog, Reset main to …? , asks how to treat the changes in the commits that leave the branch. Choose Soft (their changes stay staged), Mixed (their changes stay, unstaged) or Hard (their changes and your uncommitted work are thrown away). Hard asks once more, under Throw away uncommitted work? , because that part can't be recovered. Soft keeps the changes staged, Mixed keeps them unstaged, Hard throws them away. The commits after the one you chose leave the branch, but Undo can bring them back right afterwards. After a Hard reset the commits can come back that way, but the uncommitted edits that were thrown away cannot. Undo a commit you already shared: revert # If the commit is on the remote, don't rewrite history. Select it on History and click Revert , then Revert again in the dialog. That adds a new commit that undoes its changes and keeps the old one in the history, so nobody else's copy breaks. Throw away uncommitted edits on purpose # On Changes , select the file and click Discard… . The dialog, Discard changes? , says what will be lost. A new file is deleted (the button says Delete file ); for a changed file, your unstaged edits are thrown away and staged changes stay. Undo cannot bring this back, because git never saved those edits. What git runs # Undo and the reset buttons are git commands on your branch. After a commit, Undo uses --soft . For everything else it uses --keep , and falls back to --mixed if --keep refuses because your edits touch files that differ. $ git reset --soft <previous commit> $ git reset --keep <previous commit> $ git switch <branch you came from> $ git reset --soft|--mixed|--hard <commit> $ git revert --no-edit <commit> $ git restore --worktree -- <file> $ git clean -f -d -- <new file> The last two are Discard: restore for a changed file, clean to delete a new one. The difference between reset and revert: reset moves the branch back, so the later commits leave it. revert keeps them and adds a commit that cancels one out. If something goes wrong # \"Nothing to undo yet.\" There is no earlier step recorded for this repository. Use Reset to here… on History instead. You chose Hard by mistake. Click Undo to bring the commits back, but un"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/conflicts.html","title":"Resolve a merge conflict","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"When a merge, rebase or cherry-pick stops because both sides changed the same lines.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A conflict happens when two branches changed the same lines of the same file and git can't tell which version to keep. It stops in the middle of a merge, rebase, cherry-pick or revert and asks you. Nothing is broken and nothing is lost; you decide, and then git carries on. In the conflicted file, git marks the two versions like this: <<<<<<< HEAD the version on your current branch (ours) ======= the version from the other branch (theirs) >>>>>>> other-branch Resolving means ending up with the text you want, with all three marker lines gone. The two names matter for the Keep mine (ours) and Take theirs buttons below. In a merge , \"ours\" is the branch you are on and \"theirs\" is the one you are merging in. In a rebase they are the other way round: \"ours\" is the branch you are rebasing onto, and \"theirs\" is your own commit being replayed. In canopy desktop # When an operation stops on conflicts, Changes shows a banner ( A merge is in progress. , or rebase, cherry-pick or revert) and a Conflicts section listing each file. The banner says how many conflicts are left. Click a conflicted file. The right side explains what happened and offers four buttons. To choose line by line: click Open in editor , keep what you want between the markers, delete the marker lines, save, and click Mark resolved . To choose one whole side: click Keep mine (ours) or Take theirs . That replaces the file with that version and marks it resolved in one step. When the Conflicts list is empty, click Continue merge in the banner (it says rebase, cherry-pick or revert when that is what is in progress). The button is greyed out until every conflict is resolved. To give up, click Abort and confirm. Everything goes back to how it was before the operation started. The banner has Abort and Continue merge. The buttons decide what happens to the selected file. Mark resolved doesn't check the file. canopy stages whatever is in the file when you click it. Make sure the <<<<<<< , ======= and >>>>>>> lines are gone first, or they end up in your commit. Open in editor uses the editor set in Settings (General). Change it there if the wrong program opens. What git runs # Choosing a whole side is the first two lines. Marking a file resolved is just the second. Continue and Abort use the command that matches the operation in progress. $ git checkout --ours -- <file> # or --theirs $ git add -- <file> $ git -c core.editor=true merge --continue $ git merge --abort $ git -c core.editor=true rebase --continue $ git rebase --abort For a cherry-pick or a revert, canopy runs cherry-pick or revert with --continue or --abort instead. core.editor=true keeps git from stopping to ask for a commit message. If something goes wrong # Continue is greyed out. A file is still listed under Conflicts . Resolve it with one of the four buttons and the button turns on. Markers ended up in the file. Mark resolved doesn't check for them. Open the file, remove the marker lines, and stage it again from Changes . If you have already continued, fix the file and make a new commit or amend (see Fix your last commit ). Keep mine kept the wrong side. During a rebase, \"ours\" and \"theirs\" swap (see the top of this page). Click the other button. The file is already marked resolved, so open it from the Staged list and edit it there, or abort and start again. It went badly and you want to start over. Click Abort . Nothing is lost. After the merge is finished, you can still take it back with the undo arrow: see Undo a mistake . You'd like fewer conflicts. Pull often and keep branches short. See Branches, merging and rebasing . Related pages # Branches, merging and rebasing Sync with a remote Undo a mistake Put work aside with the stash Resolve a merge conflict in canopy console (pick each conflict from the keyboard)"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/stash.html","title":"Put work aside with the stash","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Save half-done changes without committing them, switch to something else, and bring them back later.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"put-aside","text":"Put changes aside"},{"id":"bring-back","text":"Bring it back"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"The stash is a shelf for half-done work. You have changes you are not ready to commit, but you need a clean folder right now: to switch branches, pull, or fix something urgent. Stash them, do the other thing, then bring them back. A stash is stored inside your repository (not sent to GitHub), and you can keep several. Each one is named stash@{0} , stash@{1} and so on, newest first. The Stash page in the sidebar shows a count of how many you have. In canopy desktop # Put changes aside # Open Stash in the sidebar and click Stash changes… . In the Stash changes dialog, give it a name so you recognise it later (optional, for example half-done refactor ). Include new (untracked) files is ticked for you when there are brand-new files; leave it ticked to put those aside too. Click Stash . Your files go back to how they were at the last commit, and the stash appears in the list. If nothing has changed, canopy says Nothing to stash: the working tree is clean. Bring it back # Open Stash and click the stash in the list. The right side shows exactly what is in it. Click Pop to bring the changes back and remove the stash, or Apply to bring them back and keep the stash (handy to try the same changes on another branch). Click Drop… and then Drop to delete a stash you don't need any more. Pick a stash to see what is in it, then Pop, Apply or Drop. Drop can't be undone. canopy's undo arrow covers commits, checkouts and resets, not stashes. Check the name in the confirm dialog ( Drop stash@{0}? ) before you click Drop . What git runs # The stash buttons run plain git. --include-untracked is what puts brand-new files aside too, and -m is the name you typed. $ git stash push --include-untracked -m \"half-done refactor\" $ git stash apply stash@{0} $ git stash pop stash@{0} $ git stash drop stash@{0} If something goes wrong # Apply or Pop stops with an error about local changes. Git won't bring a stash back over files you have changed since. Commit those changes or stash them first, then try again. Nothing was lost; the stash is still in the list. Pop leaves conflicts. The stash and your current files changed the same lines. When that happens git keeps the stash, so nothing is lost. Fix the files on Changes and drop the stash when you are done. See Resolve a merge conflict . The notice says something like \"Popped stash@{0} failed\". That is the error toast for a failed Pop, and the reason follows in the notice. The stash is still in the list, so read the reason, fix it, and try again. The stash is on the wrong branch. Stashes are not tied to a branch. Switch to the right branch and click Apply there. You dropped one by mistake. canopy can't bring it back. Git usually still holds the content for a while, but finding it takes advanced git commands, so double-check the name before you drop. You popped and want it back on the shelf. Click Stash changes… again. Undo a mistake covers commits and resets, not stashes. Related pages # Branches, merging and rebasing Resolve a merge conflict Undo a mistake Work on two branches at once (canopy console: switch without stashing)"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/ignore-files.html","title":"Ignore files with .gitignore","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Keep build output, secrets and editor clutter out of git, and stop tracking a file you committed by mistake.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"patterns","text":"Patterns you'll use"},{"id":"untrack","text":"Stop tracking a file you already committed"},{"id":"global","text":"Ignore files on every project"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Some files should never be in git: build output, downloaded packages such as node_modules , secrets like .env , and clutter your computer makes ( .DS_Store ). A file named .gitignore at the top of your project lists what git should leave alone. Ignored files stop showing up in your changes and can't be staged by accident. Ignoring only works for files git is not tracking yet. If you already committed a file, ignoring it is not enough: see Stop tracking a file you already committed . In canopy desktop # canopy desktop has no \"ignore this file\" button. What it does offer: For a new repository: when you set one up (see Start a new repository ), the .gitignore drop-down can start you with a ready-made list for Rust, Node / JavaScript, Python or macOS only (.DS_Store) . The default is No .gitignore . For an existing repository: open the project's .gitignore in any text editor (create it in the top folder of the project if it doesn't exist) and add one pattern per line, for example node_modules/ or .env . Back in canopy, open Changes . The ignored files are gone from the list, and .gitignore itself shows up as a change. Stage it and commit it so your teammates get the same list. When you create a repository, the .gitignore drop-down starts you with a ready-made list. What git runs # Adding to .gitignore is a text edit, not a git command, and git reads the file on its own the next time it looks at your files. The only command involved is the one that stops tracking a file you committed earlier, which you run yourself (see below): $ git rm --cached -- path/to/file Patterns you'll use # Line Ignores .env Any file called .env , in any folder /target Only target at the top of the project (a leading / means \"from the top\") node_modules/ Any folder called node_modules (a trailing / means folder) *.log Every file ending in .log !keep.log An exception: don't ignore this one # a note A comment Stop tracking a file you already committed # If a file was committed before you ignored it, git keeps tracking it, and .gitignore has no effect on it. canopy desktop can't do this step, so you use a terminal: Add the file to .gitignore as above. In a terminal, inside the repository, run git rm --cached path/to/file (use git rm -r --cached folder for a folder). This makes git forget the file without deleting it from your disk. Back in canopy, the deletion shows up on Changes as a staged change. Commit it. From then on the file stays on your disk but out of git. Secrets stay in history. This stops tracking the file from now on, but earlier commits still contain it. If it held a password or a key, treat that secret as leaked and change it. Ignore files on every project # For clutter that comes from your own computer and editor, rather than the project, a global ignore file saves editing every repository. In a terminal: $ git config --global core.excludesFile ~/.gitignore_global Then list patterns such as .DS_Store in ~/.gitignore_global . This is plain git, not something canopy sets up for you. If something goes wrong # The file still shows up after you ignored it. It is already tracked. Follow Stop tracking a file you already committed . A pattern ignores too much. Make it more specific (start with / to anchor it at the top of the project) or add an exception with ! . Then look at Changes to see what came back. You need to commit an ignored file once. Ignored files don't appear on Changes , so canopy can't stage them. Remove or narrow its pattern first, or run git add -f file in a terminal. You added a wrong line to .gitignore . The undo arrow doesn't cover edits to files. Open .gitignore in your editor and delete the line, or use Discard… on Changes to throw away all your edits to it. Related pages # Start a new repository Write good commits Undo a mistake Troubleshooting Ignore files in canopy console (has an ignore key)"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/tags-releases.html","title":"Tag a version and publish a release","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Mark a commit as v1.2.0, push the tag, and publish a GitHub release with notes.","headings":[{"id":"tag-or-release","text":"Tag or release?"},{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"other-tag-buttons","text":"The other buttons on a tag"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A tag is a permanent name for one commit, such as v1.2.0 . Unlike a branch, it never moves. People use tags to mark the exact code that shipped, so they can come back to it later. canopy desktop can create a tag, push it to your remote, check it out and delete it. It cannot create a GitHub release: that part happens on github.com, after the tag is pushed. Tag or release? # Thing What it is Where you make it Tag A name on one commit. Part of your git history, so it travels with the repository. canopy desktop (History page) Release A GitHub page built on a tag, with notes and downloads. github.com, on the tag you pushed Lightweight or annotated. If you leave the message empty, canopy makes a lightweight tag: just a name on a commit. If you type a message, it makes an annotated tag, which also records who tagged it, when, and your message. Annotated tags are the usual choice for versions you ship. In canopy desktop # Open History in the sidebar and click the commit you want to mark. Its details open on the right. Click Tag… . A dialog titled \"Tag\" and the commit's short id opens. Type a Tag name , such as v1.2.0 . Add a message in Message (leave empty for a lightweight tag) if you want an annotated tag, then click Create tag . A toast confirms \"Tagged … as v1.2.0\", and the tag shows as a chip on that commit. Open Branches and choose the Tags tab. Each tag lists its name, the commit subject and its age. Click Push on the tag row to send it to your remote. This button only shows when the repository has a remote. The toast reads \"Pushed tag v1.2.0 to origin\". To publish a release, open the pushed tag on GitHub in your browser and create the release from it, with your notes. canopy desktop has no release button. Select a commit in History, then click Tag…. The v0.1.0 chip on the older commit is a tag. The Tags tab lists every tag. Hover a row to see Check out, Push and Delete. The other buttons on a tag # Check out switches your working folder to that tag's code. Git puts you in a \"detached HEAD\" state, which means you are not on a branch. Look around, then switch back to a branch on the Branches page. Don't commit there without making a branch first ( New branch here… in History does that). Delete asks \"Delete tag …?\" and says: \"Deletes it here only; a pushed tag stays on the remote.\" The tag on your remote is not touched. What git runs # Pushing goes to origin , or to the first remote if there is no origin . The releases step happens on GitHub, so no command appears for it. $ git tag v1.2.0 <commit> $ git tag -a v1.2.0 -m \"what changed\" <commit> $ git push origin v1.2.0 $ git tag -d v1.2.0 The first line is used when the message is empty, the second when you typed one. Check out runs git switch v1.2.0 . If something goes wrong # The tag name already exists. Git refuses to reuse a tag name. Pick a new name, such as v1.2.1 , or delete the old tag first on the Tags tab. There is no Push button. The repository has no remote yet. Connect one first (see Start a new repository , or Sync with a remote ). You tagged the wrong commit, and it was not pushed yet. Delete it on the Tags tab, then tag the right commit from History. Nothing else is affected. You tagged the wrong commit, and you already pushed it. Delete only removes the tag on your computer. Delete it on GitHub too (the repository's tags list), then delete it here and push the corrected tag. If others may have fetched the old one, use a new name instead. The release shows the wrong code. A release belongs to a tag, and a tag belongs to one commit. Check which commit the tag points at in History. If it is wrong, replace the tag as above, then recreate the release on GitHub. You checked out a tag and your commits seem to vanish. You were in a detached HEAD. Read Undo a mistake to get them back. Related pages # Sync with a remote Working with GitHub Branches, merging and rebasing Undo a mistake Git in ten minutes"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/review-a-pr.html","title":"Review a pull request","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Read a pull request, comment, and approve or ask for changes.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"merge-choices","text":"Merge choices"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A pull request (PR) is a proposal to merge one branch into another, usually someone else's work into main . Reviewing it means reading what it does, leaving comments, and then approving it or asking for changes. canopy desktop shows the pull requests waiting for you, lets you check a branch out to try it, and submits your review. It does not show the diff itself, so to read the changed lines use View in GitHub . You need GitHub connected first (see Working with GitHub ). In canopy desktop # Open Pull requests in the sidebar, under GitHub. The tabs are Open , Mine , To review and All . Choose To review to see only the ones waiting for you. Drafts carry a \"draft\" label. On Home, the GitHub card also says how many pull requests are waiting for your review. Click a pull request. The details show the title, who wants to merge which branch into which, the number of lines added and removed, the review decision, labels, the description, the checks (\"Checks 3/3 passed\", each with a details link), the reviews so far and the comments. To read the changed code, click View in GitHub . To run it yourself, click Check out : canopy switches your working folder to that branch. To just say something, click Comment… , type your text and click Comment . To give your verdict, click Review… . In \"Your review\" pick Approve , Request changes or Comment only . Write your note in \"Comment (needed unless approving)\" and click Submit review . To finish the pull request, click Merge… (see the choices below). To abandon it, click Close… . The Pull requests page. The buttons under the title are View in GitHub, Check out, Comment…, Review…, Close… and Merge…. The review dialog. Approving needs no comment; the other two do. Merge choices # Merge… asks \"Merge #N into main?\" with a How choice and a box \"Delete branch afterwards\", which is ticked by default. Choice What lands on the target branch Squash and merge (one commit) All the pull request's commits become one new commit. This is the default and keeps history tidy. Create a merge commit Every commit is kept, plus one commit that joins the two branches. Rebase and merge Every commit is kept and replayed on top of the target branch, with no join commit. Conflicts. If the branch clashes with its target, Merge… shows a warning tooltip and a banner says \"Conflicts with main. Check it out, merge main into it, resolve in Changes, then push.\" Resolve a merge conflict shows how. Close is not delete. Closing asks \"Close #N without merging?\" and says \"You can reopen it on GitHub later.\" The branch itself stays. What git runs # canopy talks to GitHub through the gh command line tool, so these are the commands behind the buttons. <n> is the pull request number. $ gh pr checkout <n> $ gh pr comment <n> --body \"...\" $ gh pr review <n> --approve $ gh pr review <n> --request-changes --body \"...\" $ gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch $ gh pr close <n> Comment only uses --comment . Merge uses --merge , --squash or --rebase to match your choice, and --delete-branch only when its box is ticked. If something goes wrong # The page says \"Log in to GitHub\". The GitHub CLI is installed but not signed in. Run gh auth login in a terminal, then reopen the page. \"Add a comment to explain what should change.\" Request changes and Comment only need text. Only Approve can go without it. Check out fails. You have uncommitted changes that would be overwritten. Put them aside first (see Put work aside with the stash ), then check out again. To go back to your own branch afterwards, use the Branches page. Merge fails. The branch has conflicts, a required check has not passed, or a required review is missing. Read the banner and the checks list first. A check is red. Click its details link, or see Fix a failing CI run . You merged the wrong pull request. A merge is a normal commit on the target branch. Undo a mistake shows how to take it back. You closed one by accident. Reopen it on GitHub, as the close dialog says. Related pages # Worki"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/fix-ci.html","title":"Fix a failing CI run","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Find out why CI failed, fix it (or re-run it), and get back to green.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"assistant","text":"Choosing the AI assistant"},{"id":"given","text":"What the assistant is given"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"CI (continuous integration) is the robot on GitHub that builds and tests your code every time you push. When a run fails, the commit gets a red mark, and the cause is buried in a long log. canopy desktop watches the latest run for the commit you are on. If it failed, Home shows a banner with the short reason. From there you can read the failed part of the log, re-run the failed jobs, or hand the error to an AI assistant that opens in a terminal window. You need GitHub connected first (see Working with GitHub ). In canopy desktop # Open Home . If the latest run for your current commit on this branch failed, a banner says \"canopy found an error in your latest commit\", with the headline of the error and the workflow name. canopy checks again whenever your branch moves, and every minute. Click Details on the banner. A window titled \"What went wrong\" shows the same text an assistant would get, and the file it is saved in. Copy copies it, Open in editor opens it, and View in GitHub opens the run in your browser. To see every run, open Actions in the sidebar. The tabs are This branch and All branches . Each row shows the workflow, the branch, what triggered it and its age. Click a run. The jobs are listed with their steps, and failed ones start open. The end of the failed log is shown under \"End of the failed log\". Fix it yourself, or click Fix with your assistant (its name is on the button, for example Fix with Claude Code ). canopy saves a prompt file and opens the assistant in a new terminal window. The toast says where it opened. Review what the assistant changed on the Changes page. Commit and push the fix (see Write good commits ). A new run starts on its own. If the failure was a fluke (a network hiccup, a slow test), click Re-run failed jobs on the run's page instead of pushing anything. The banner on Home appears when the latest run for your commit has failed. The Actions page. A failed run offers Details, Re-run failed jobs and Fix with your assistant. What git runs # These commands go through the gh command line tool. <id> is the run's number. $ gh run view <id> --json jobs $ gh run view <id> --log-failed $ gh run rerun <id> --failed Committing and pushing your fix are ordinary git commit and git push commands, run from the Changes page. Choosing the AI assistant # In Settings , the \"AI assistant\" row has these choices. Choice What it does Auto Uses whichever assistant canopy finds on your computer. Claude Code, Codex Always uses that one. Custom command… Runs your own command in a \"Command\" field. Put {prompt} where the prompt should go, for example aider --message {prompt} . Without {prompt} , the prompt is added at the end. Off No assistant. The Fix button becomes \"Set up an AI assistant\". What the assistant is given # canopy writes a prompt to .git/canopy/fix-ci.md inside your repository. It holds the failed job and step, GitHub's summary of the failure, and the last 200 lines of the failed log with the noise cleaned out. It ends with the instruction not to commit or push, so you review the change first. Your code may leave your computer. The assistant sends what it reads to its own service. The log can contain file names, paths and messages from your project. Read Details before you click Fix with if that matters for this repository. If something goes wrong # \"No AI assistant set up yet\". Nothing is installed or chosen. Install Claude Code or Codex, or pick or type one in Settings, which the button opens for you. \"Couldn't open a terminal. The prompt is saved in …\". Open the saved file and paste it into your assistant yourself. \"macOS didn't let canopy open …\". Allow it in System Settings, Privacy & Security, Automation: turn on Terminal (or iTerm) under canopy. Then try again. The prompt file is saved either way. The banner never appears. It only shows when the latest run for exactly this commit, on this branch, failed. If you have pushed a newer commit, the run may still be going. It also needs GitHub connected. It "},{"url":"wiki/desktop/new-repo.html","title":"Start a new repository","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Turn a folder into a git repository, then put it on GitHub over HTTPS or SSH.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy desktop"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"https-or-ssh","text":"HTTPS or SSH"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A repository is a folder that git keeps the history of. Every project starts by turning a folder into one, and most people then put a copy on GitHub so it is backed up and easy to share. canopy desktop does both in one short setup: it creates the repository, adds the starter files you choose, makes the first commit, and can create the GitHub repository and push to it. Each step can be skipped. In canopy desktop # On the welcome screen, click Start a new one… and pick a folder. An empty folder or a folder full of files both work. If you use Open a repository… ( ⌘O , Ctrl+O on Windows and Linux) on a folder that is not a repository yet, the same setup starts. Step 1 says \"There's no git repository here yet\" and shows the folder. Files already in it are left as they are. Click Create a repository here , or Choose another folder . Step 2, \"Set up\", asks for: Branch name : your git setting for new repositories, or main if you have none. Add a README.md : a page that says what the project is. GitHub shows it on the project's front page. Ticked by default. .gitignore : files git should leave alone, like build output. Choose No .gitignore, Rust, Node / JavaScript, Python, or macOS only (.DS_Store). Add an MIT license : lets others use your code. Not ticked by default. Make the first commit (\"Initial commit\") : ticked by default. Click Create repository . Step 3, \"Connect it to GitHub (optional)\", has two tabs. Create it on GitHub only appears when you are signed in with the GitHub CLI. Give it a \"Name on GitHub\", choose Private or Public, and click Create and push . The other tab, Use an existing repository , is described next. For an existing GitHub repository, make it on github.com first, without a README. Then type Repository as owner/name or paste its URL, choose how git connects (HTTPS or SSH, see below), check the \"Address\" line and click Connect and push . To do this later, click Skip for now . The last screen says your folder is ready, lists what ran, and has an Open it button. The welcome screen. Start a new one… begins the setup. Step 2 chooses the starter files and the first commit. Step 3 connects the repository to GitHub, or you skip it. Already have a repository with no GitHub copy? On Home, use the action Connect to GitHub… . It opens only step 3. What git runs # The last screen prints these lines so you can repeat them by hand next time. Files such as the README are only written if they are missing, and the commit is skipped when the folder is empty. $ git init -b main $ git add --all $ git commit -m \"Initial commit\" $ gh repo create my-project --private --source=. --remote origin --push If you connect to an existing repository instead of creating one, the last line becomes: $ git remote add origin <address> $ git push --progress --set-upstream origin main With an older git that lacks init -b , canopy runs git init and then names the branch. If a remote called origin already exists, its address is updated instead of added, so trying again is safe. --push and the push only happen when there is a commit. HTTPS or SSH # Both reach the same GitHub repository. They differ in how git proves who you are. HTTPS SSH Address looks like https://github.com/owner/name.git git@github.com:owner/name.git Sign-in Through the GitHub CLI or your computer's saved credentials. A key on your computer that GitHub knows. Best when You have signed in with gh auth login . You already have an SSH key set up. Which one is suggested. canopy marks one as \"suggested\". It follows your GitHub CLI setting, or picks SSH when it finds a key on your computer. The SSH choice says whether a key was found on this computer. If something goes wrong # \"git doesn't know your name and email yet, so it can't commit.\" Set them once in a terminal, then create the repository again: git config --global user.name \"Your Name\" and git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\" . \"Added the remote, but the push didn't go through.\" The GitHub repository probably does not"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/keys.html","title":"Keyboard shortcuts","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Every keyboard shortcut in canopy desktop, on macOS, Windows and Linux.","headings":[{"id":"everywhere","text":"Anywhere in the window"},{"id":"changes","text":"On the Changes page"},{"id":"history","text":"On the History page"},{"id":"dialogs","text":"In dialogs"},{"id":"missing","text":"What has no shortcut"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"canopy desktop is a mouse-first app, so it has a short list of shortcuts: a few for the whole window and a few for lists. Everything else is a button. On macOS the shortcuts use ⌘ (Command); on Windows and Linux the same shortcuts use Ctrl . canopy console has many more, because it is driven entirely from the keyboard. Its keys are different on purpose: see the canopy console keys . Anywhere in the window # macOS Windows and Linux What it does ⌘O Ctrl+O Open a repository (a folder picker). ⌘R Ctrl+R Refresh: look at the repository again. ⌘Z Ctrl+Z Undo the last commit, reset or checkout. It shows what it will do and asks first. It only works when you aren't typing in a text box, so it doesn't get in the way of undoing text. ⌘, Ctrl+, Open Settings . ⌘1 to ⌘9 Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+9 Go to a page, counting down the sidebar (below). Only once a repository is open. The number shortcuts follow the order of the sidebar: Key Page Key Page ⌘1 Home ⌘6 Pull requests ⌘2 Changes ⌘7 Issues ⌘3 History ⌘8 Actions ⌘4 Branches ⌘9 Notifications ⌘5 Stash On the Changes page # Key What it does ↓ or j Select the next file. ↑ or k Select the previous file. space Stage the selected file, or unstage it if it is already staged. It does nothing for a file in conflict: resolve those first (see resolve a merge conflict ). ⌘↵ ( Ctrl+↵ ) Commit, from the summary or description box. The commit button shows this shortcut. On the History page # Key What it does ↓ or j Select the next (older) commit. ↑ or k Select the previous (newer) commit. / Jump to the search box. The single keys on these two pages only work when you aren't typing in a text box, and only when no dialog is open. In dialogs # esc closes a dialog without doing anything, the same as Cancel . It closes Settings and your account panel too, and it leaves the new-repository steps unless canopy is in the middle of something. While a dialog is open, all the shortcuts above are switched off, so a stray key can't act on the page behind it. What has no shortcut # Fetch, Pull, Push, stash, branch and merge actions and the GitHub buttons are all clicks: there is no shortcut for them in canopy desktop, and no command palette. If you'd rather drive everything from the keyboard, use canopy console , which has a key for every action. You can use both on the same repository. Text boxes. In a text box, the usual keys of your system work as always ( ⌘C , ⌘V , ⌘A and so on). The single-key shortcuts above are turned off while you type. Related pages # A tour of canopy desktop Settings Undo a mistake Your daily workflow canopy console keys"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/settings.html","title":"Settings","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Every setting in canopy desktop's Settings page, what it does, and its default.","headings":[{"id":"general","text":"General"},{"id":"console","text":"canopy console"},{"id":"appearance","text":"Appearance"},{"id":"updates","text":"Updates"},{"id":"about","text":"About"},{"id":"account","text":"Your GitHub account"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Open Settings with the gear at the bottom of the sidebar, or with ⌘, (Ctrl+, on Windows and Linux). The gear is on the welcome screen too, so you can change things before you open a repository. Settings has four sections down the left: General, Appearance, Updates and About. Every change is saved as soon as you make it; there is no Save button (except when you make a theme, below). The settings live in ~/.canopy/desktop.json , next to your recent repositories. Delete the file to go back to the defaults. General # General: the settings you'll change most often. Setting What it does Default Theme Shows the current theme. Appearance… jumps to the Appearance section. Auto Text editor What Open in editor uses on Changes and in commit details. Pick System default , an editor canopy found on your computer, or Custom command… and type a command line (for example idea --line 1 ); canopy adds the file at the end. System default Show git commands After each action, the message shows the git command canopy ran, so you learn git as you go. On Refresh How often canopy also looks for changes made outside it: 2s, 5s, 15s, 1m or Off. It always refreshes when you switch back to its window, and instantly when it sees a file or branch change (see live refresh ). 5s Pull What Pull does when your branch and the remote both have new commits: Default follows your git config ( pull.rebase ), Merge always merges, Rebase always rebases. Default AI assistant Which assistant Fix with AI opens when CI fails: Auto (the first one installed), Claude Code , Codex , Custom command… or Off . For a custom command, {prompt} marks where the prompt goes; without it, the prompt is added at the end. The row lists which assistants were found on your computer. Auto Recent repositories Clear list empties the list on the welcome screen. It doesn't touch any repository. New to git? The short introduction on Home. Show it again brings it back after you dismissed it. Shown until dismissed The editors canopy looks for are Visual Studio Code, Cursor, Zed, Sublime Text, Neovim, Vim, Helix, nano and Emacs. Editors that run in a terminal (Neovim, Vim, Helix, nano) open in a new terminal window. If none are found, the row says \"No editors found on your PATH\" and you can still use a custom command. canopy console # The end of General is about canopy console , the keyboard app: If it isn't installed, a card shows the command that installs it on your system, with a Copy button. If it is, the card shows its version. Suggest canopy console lets canopy mention it now and then, at most every two weeks and never in your first week. Turn it off and it never does. It is on by default, and only speaks up when canopy console isn't installed. Share settings moves four settings between the two apps: the theme, the text editor, the AI assistant and Show git commands. Import from canopy console reads its config.toml ; Send to canopy console writes into it, changing only those keys. If canopy console has no config yet, importing tells you so. Settings file saves those same settings as a JSON file ( Export… ) for another computer or for canopy --import-settings , or loads one ( Import… ). Appearance # Pick a theme by clicking its card. Auto follows your computer's light or dark setting. The built-in themes are canopy (dark), canopy light, catppuccin, gruvbox and nord. Themes you made appear under Your themes . Customize… (or Edit… for a theme of your own) opens the editor: a name, and a color picker plus hex box for each color, grouped as Base, Highlights, Changes and Git. The app changes as you pick, and a small preview of canopy console shows the colors that only appear there. Save theme stores only the colors that differ from the theme you started from. Edit JSON (for a theme of your own) opens the selected theme's file in your text editor. Save the file and come back; canopy picks up the change. Export… and Import… move a theme file in and out. Delete asks first, then removes the file. Both app"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/highlighting.html","title":"Syntax highlighting and live refresh","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"How canopy desktop colors code in diffs and notices changes as you make them.","headings":[{"id":"highlighting","text":"Syntax highlighting"},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits"},{"id":"refresh","text":"Live refresh"},{"id":"timer","text":"Refresh timer and window focus"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Two things in canopy desktop happen without you asking: code in diffs is colored by language, and the pages update themselves when files or branches change. This page explains what each one covers and where the limits are. Syntax highlighting # Diffs on the Changes, History and Stash pages are colored by language: keywords, strings, comments, numbers and so on. The green and red backgrounds for added and removed lines stay; the syntax colors sit on top of them. The colors come from your theme , so they follow it when you switch. Always on. The desktop app has no setting to turn highlighting off. (canopy console does: see its highlighting page .) Unknown languages stay plain. A file type canopy doesn't recognise, and binary files, show without syntax colors. The diff still works. Both sides are read separately. The old version and the new version of a file are each colored on their own, so a change that starts inside a string or comment still looks right. Limits # Coloring very large diffs would make the app slow, so canopy skips it when it would cost too much. The diff is still shown in full, just without syntax colors. Limit Value What happens past it Line length 2,000 characters That line is shown plain (minified files are the usual cause). Hunk size 5,000 lines That whole hunk is shown plain. Live refresh # You don't have to press refresh after you save a file in your editor, switch branches in a terminal or commit from another tool. canopy watches the repository and updates the page you're on. It reacts to: Files in your working folder , except ones git ignores (build output and the like, see ignore files ). Git's own state : the current branch, the staging area, branches and tags, and a merge, rebase, cherry-pick or revert in progress. It waits about a quarter of a second after the last change so a burst of saves (a build, a branch switch) causes one update, not fifty. The update is quiet: the page redraws in place without a spinner and without losing your selection. Git's temporary lock files and internal object files are ignored, so a running git command doesn't trigger a refresh loop. Refresh timer and window focus # Two more refreshes back this up, for changes the watcher can't see, such as a push from another computer or a new pull request: When you come back to the window. Switching to canopy from another app refreshes it. On a timer. Set it in Settings under Refresh : 2s, 5s (the default), 15s, 1m or Off. The timer only runs while the window is visible. Off doesn't turn off live refresh. The Refresh setting only controls the timer. The instant updates from the file watcher and the refresh on window focus stay on. Press ⌘R (Ctrl+R on Windows and Linux) any time to refresh by hand. If something goes wrong # A diff has no colors. Check the limits above (a very long line or a huge hunk), or the file type may not be one canopy recognises. The changes themselves are correct either way. A change doesn't show up. If git ignores the file, canopy doesn't show it. Otherwise press ⌘R . If that fixes it, see \"The page didn't update\" in troubleshooting . The app feels busy. Set Refresh to 15s, 1m or Off in Settings. Live updates from the file watcher still work. Related pages # Settings Themes Troubleshooting Highlighting and live refresh in canopy console"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/troubleshooting.html","title":"Troubleshooting","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Fixes for the problems people run into with canopy desktop.","headings":[{"id":"opening","text":"Opening the app"},{"id":"unsigned","text":"macOS says the app can't be opened"},{"id":"smartscreen","text":"Windows shows a blue SmartScreen warning"},{"id":"git-missing","text":"\"git wasn't found\""},{"id":"github","text":"GitHub pages"},{"id":"macos-automation","text":"Fix with AI or Open in editor does nothing (macOS)"},{"id":"stale","text":"Changes and history"},{"id":"no-update","text":"The page didn't update"},{"id":"missing-file","text":"A file I changed isn't listed"},{"id":"nothing-to-commit","text":"The commit button says \"Nothing to commit\""},{"id":"syncing","text":"Fetch, pull and push"},{"id":"conflicts","text":"Merges and conflicts"},{"id":"undo","text":"I made a mistake"},{"id":"shortcuts","text":"A shortcut does nothing"},{"id":"updating","text":"Updating"},{"id":"report","text":"Still stuck"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"The problems people run into with canopy desktop, grouped by where they show up. Each one says what you'll see and what to do. canopy never changes history without asking first, so most of these are about setup rather than lost work. If your problem isn't here, see the end of the page. Opening the app # macOS says the app can't be opened # canopy desktop isn't signed or notarized by Apple (and won't be), so macOS asks you to confirm it the first time. Open System Settings , go to Privacy & Security , scroll down and press Open Anyway next to the message about canopy, then open the app again. Or remove the quarantine flag from a terminal: $ xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/canopy.app Windows shows a blue SmartScreen warning # Same reason. Choose More info , then Run anyway . On Linux there is no such prompt. \"git wasn't found\" # canopy runs your own git, so it needs git installed. The account panel (click your name at the bottom of the sidebar) shows the git version canopy found, or says it wasn't found. Install git, quit canopy and open it again. GitHub pages # The pull requests, issues, Actions and notifications pages need the GitHub CLI ( gh ). When something is missing, the page shows a setup card instead of a list. The GitHub CLI isn't installed. The card shows the command for your system ( brew install gh on macOS, winget install --id GitHub.cli -e on Windows) or a GitHub CLI website button. Install it, then press Try again . You aren't logged in. Run gh auth login in a terminal, follow the prompts, then press Try again . canopy never sees your token; it only asks gh . \"Not a GitHub repository.\" The folder's remote isn't on GitHub, or it has no remote yet. Every git page still works; only the GitHub ones are unavailable. See start a new repository to connect one. Wrong account. The account panel shows who gh is logged in as. Run gh auth logout then gh auth login to switch, and press Check again . The whole story is on working with GitHub . Fix with AI or Open in editor does nothing (macOS) # These open a new Terminal window for your AI assistant or a terminal editor, and macOS asks once whether canopy may control Terminal. If you pressed Don't Allow , canopy shows a message saying it was denied. Turn it back on in System Settings , Privacy & Security , Automation , and switch on Terminal under canopy. Then try again. If nothing opens even with permission, open Settings and check the AI assistant row lists one you have installed. For Fix with AI, canopy saves the prompt it built to .git/canopy/fix-ci.md inside the repository, so you can paste it into any assistant by hand. Changes and history # The page didn't update # canopy watches for changes and refreshes by itself (see live refresh ). If it lags, press ⌘R (Ctrl+R on Windows and Linux). If that always fixes it, your repository may be on a network drive or a synced folder (Dropbox, iCloud, OneDrive) that doesn't report changes; set Refresh to 2s in Settings. A file I changed isn't listed # It is probably ignored by git (in .gitignore ), so git doesn't track it. See ignore files . Also check you opened the right repository. The commit button says \"Nothing to commit\" # Nothing is staged. Stage files with the + next to each one, Stage all , or press space on a selected one. See write good commits . Fetch, pull and push # \"Nothing to push.\" Your branch has no commits the remote lacks. Nothing is wrong. \"This branch isn't on the remote yet.\" Use the Push button: canopy publishes the branch for you. Push is rejected, or the Push dialog offers three choices. The remote has commits you don't have. Choose Pull with merge or Pull with rebase , then push again. Force push overwrites the remote, so only use it on a branch of your own that you rewrote on purpose; canopy uses --force-with-lease , which refuses if someone else pushed in the meantime. See sync with a remote . Asked for a password, or authentication fails. git needs credentials for the remote. Run gh auth lo"},{"url":"wiki/desktop/faq.html","title":"FAQ","section":"canopy desktop","lede":"Short answers to common questions about canopy desktop.","headings":[{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing and installing"},{"id":"which-app","text":"Which app should I use?"},{"id":"install","text":"How do I install it?"},{"id":"unsigned","text":"Why does my computer say the app isn't trusted?"},{"id":"update","text":"How do I update it?"},{"id":"using","text":"Using it"},{"id":"shortcuts","text":"What shortcuts does it have?"},{"id":"undo","text":"Can I undo a mistake?"},{"id":"commands","text":"How do I see the git commands it runs?"},{"id":"highlighting","text":"Can I turn off the colors in diffs, or the automatic refresh?"},{"id":"ai","text":"Can an AI assistant help fix a failing CI run?"},{"id":"both","text":"About canopy in general"},{"id":"replace-git","text":"Does canopy replace git?"},{"id":"safe","text":"Is it safe to use on important repositories?"},{"id":"privacy","text":"Does it send my code anywhere?"},{"id":"other-hosts","text":"Can I use it with GitLab or Bitbucket?"},{"id":"settings-shared","text":"Do canopy desktop and canopy console share settings?"},{"id":"license","text":"What does it cost, and what's the license?"},{"id":"bug-report","text":"How do I report a bug?"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Short answers to common questions about canopy desktop. If something is not working, try Troubleshooting . Choosing and installing # Which app should I use? # New to git, or you prefer a window? Use canopy desktop. It explains each git word, gives you a button for the next step, and asks before anything destructive. Want everything from the keyboard? canopy console is the power tool: interactive rebase, bisect, worktrees, every repository at once and your own commands. You can use both on the same repository; they share themes in ~/.canopy . How do I install it? # On a Mac: brew install --cask shankar-sachin/canopy/canopy-desktop . On Windows: winget install shankars.canopy-desktop . On Linux, download the .deb or AppImage from Downloads . See canopy desktop for details. Why does my computer say the app isn't trusted? # canopy desktop isn't signed or notarized by Apple, and won't be. On a Mac, open System Settings → Privacy & Security and click Open Anyway once. On Windows, SmartScreen may say it doesn't recognize the app: click More info , then Run anyway . Linux builds don't need this. How do I update it? # Settings → Updates checks for a newer version once a day when canopy starts (you can turn that off, or press Check now ) and tells you how to update for the way you installed it. On a Mac with Homebrew that is brew upgrade --cask canopy-desktop ; with winget, winget upgrade shankars.canopy-desktop . Using it # What shortcuts does it have? # A short list: open, refresh, undo, settings, and ⌘1 to ⌘9 (Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+9 on Windows and Linux) to jump between pages. See Keyboard shortcuts . Can I undo a mistake? # Most of them. The undo button in the top bar, or ⌘Z (Ctrl+Z on Windows and Linux), takes back the last commit, reset or checkout; it shows what it will do and asks first. For anything older, the reflog remembers where every branch has been. See Undo a mistake . How do I see the git commands it runs? # Turn on Show git commands in Settings. canopy then shows the command behind each action. Can I turn off the colors in diffs, or the automatic refresh? # Not the colors: highlighting is always on. The instant refresh has no off switch either, but you can slow the timer down or turn it off in Settings → General → Refresh . See Syntax highlighting and live refresh . Can an AI assistant help fix a failing CI run? # canopy can hand a failed CI run to an AI assistant you have installed, with Fix with on the Actions page or the Home banner. Pick the assistant in Settings. See Fix a failing CI run . About canopy in general # Does canopy replace git? # No. canopy runs the real git for everything, so your hooks, config, signing and credential helpers all work, and you can mix it freely with the command line. Nothing is stored in a separate format. Is it safe to use on important repositories? # canopy asks before anything destructive, and undo takes back most actions. Everything it does is a normal git command, and you can have it show you each one. Git can't restore edits you discarded with a hard reset or Discard… , so those ask first and say so. Does it send my code anywhere? # No. Git runs on your computer. The GitHub features use your own GitHub CLI ( gh ) login and only contact GitHub when you open a GitHub page or act on one. Checking for a new version only asks GitHub which version is latest. Can I use it with GitLab or Bitbucket? # All the git features work with any remote. The GitHub pages are GitHub-only for now. Do canopy desktop and canopy console share settings? # They share the theme files in ~/.canopy . In Settings → General, Share settings can copy the theme, text editor, AI assistant and git-command setting to or from canopy console, on request. Everything else is set separately: canopy console's options are in its config.toml , and canopy desktop's are in its Settings page. What does it cost, and what's the license? # canopy is free and open source under the MIT license. The code is on GitHub , and contributions a"},{"url":"wiki/console/index.html","title":"The canopy console guide","section":"canopy console","lede":"Everything in canopy console, page by page: guides for the daily loop, recipes for everyday fixes and power tools, and reference for keys and config.","headings":[{"id":"organised","text":"How this guide is organised"},{"id":"start-here","text":"Start here"},{"id":"guides","text":"Guides"},{"id":"recipes","text":"Recipes"},{"id":"reference","text":"Reference"}],"text":"The keyboard-driven half of canopy runs in your terminal. It shows your repository as ten tabs and puts the git command behind every action in the footer, so you learn as you go. This guide covers everything canopy console can do. New to git? Read Git in ten minutes first, then the tour and your daily workflow . Already comfortable with git? Jump to a recipe, or look up a key on Keyboard shortcuts . How this guide is organised # Start here is a tour of every screen and the key that opens it. Guides follow the day-to-day loop: commit, branch, sync, and work with GitHub. Recipes fix one problem each: undo a mistake, resolve a conflict, find a bug, and so on. Every guide and recipe has the same parts: the steps with their keys, the git commands that run, what to do if something goes wrong, and related pages. Reference lists every key, every config option and every command-line flag, with troubleshooting and a FAQ. Prefer clicking? The same recipes, with buttons and screenshots, are in the canopy desktop guide . Start here # A tour of canopy console Home, Changes, Log, Refs, Stash, Repos, Reflog, PRs, Issues and CI, one at a time. Guides # Your daily workflow Pull, branch, edit, stage, commit, push, open a PR: the loop you'll do every day. Write good commits Small commits, clear messages, and staging just the lines that belong together. Branches, merging and rebasing Why branches, how merging and rebasing differ, and what to do when they collide. Sync with a remote Fetch, pull and push; ahead and behind; a diverged branch; force-with-lease. Working with GitHub Connect GitHub, then pull requests, CI and notifications. Recipes # Fix your last commit Typo in the message, a forgotten file, or a fix for an older commit. Undo a mistake Undo a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back \"lost\" work. Resolve a merge conflict Pick a side (or both) for each conflict, then finish the merge or rebase. Put work aside with the stash Save half-done work, switch tasks, and bring it back later. Ignore files with .gitignore Keep build output, secrets and clutter out of git. Tidy up commits before sharing Squash, reorder, reword or drop commits with a visual interactive rebase. Find the commit that broke something Bisect to the breaking commit, then blame and file history to understand it. Work on two branches at once Check out a second branch in its own folder with worktrees. Tag a version and publish a release Tag a commit, push the tag, and publish a GitHub release. Review a pull request Read the diff, comment, then approve or request changes. Fix a failing CI run Find why CI failed, fix it, push, and re-run. Start a new repository Set up a folder, then connect GitHub over HTTPS or SSH. Reference # Keyboard shortcuts Every key, by screen. Configuration Every setting in config.toml, with its default. Command line Flags, arguments and environment variables. Syntax highlighting and live refresh Colored diffs and instant updates, and the settings. Troubleshooting Fixes for the problems people actually hit. FAQ Short answers to common questions."},{"url":"wiki/console/tour.html","title":"A tour of canopy console","section":"canopy console","lede":"Every screen of canopy console, what it shows, and the key to get there.","headings":[{"id":"moving","text":"Moving around"},{"id":"home","text":"Home (1)"},{"id":"changes","text":"Changes (2)"},{"id":"history","text":"History (3)"},{"id":"branches","text":"Branches (4)"},{"id":"stash","text":"Stash (5)"},{"id":"workspace","text":"Workspace (6)"},{"id":"reflog","text":"Reflog (7)"},{"id":"prs","text":"Pull requests (8)"},{"id":"issues","text":"Issues (9)"},{"id":"actions","text":"Actions (0)"},{"id":"palette","text":"The command palette"},{"id":"next","text":"Where to next"}],"text":"Everything in canopy console lives in one window with ten tabs. Each tab is a screen, usually with a list on the left and details on the right. This page shows every screen, what it is for, and the key that gets you there. On a narrow terminal the tab names shorten to Log, Refs, Repos, PRs and CI. The examples come from a small demo repository. Run canopy inside any git repository to see your own. Moving around # Key What it does 1 to 9 , 0 Jump to a tab: Home, Changes, History, Branches, Stash, Workspace, Reflog, Pull requests, Issues, Actions. tab and ⇧tab Next and previous tab. j k or ↓ ↑ Move down and up a list. g and G jump to the top and bottom. ↵ and esc Open the selected item, and go back. ? Every key that works on the screen you are on. : The command palette: type a few letters of any action and press ↵ . ⌃P and ⌃K open it too. T Show or hide teach mode, the footer that shows the git command behind each action. ⌃T Cycle through the themes. q Quit. The bar at the bottom always lists the most useful keys for the screen you are on. Actions that work everywhere have the same key on every tab: c commit, p pull, P push, f fetch, z undo and S stash. Home (1) # Where you are and what to do next. The top panel shows the branch, what it tracks, how far ahead or behind it is, and counts of staged, changed, conflicted and stashed files. Next steps turns that into plain advice with the key to press: \"2 file(s) staged and ready. Press c to commit.\" Recent commits with a graph are below. On a wide terminal, a GitHub card on the right shows the pull request and checks for your branch. canopy console: Home Changes (2) # Everything you have edited, in three groups: Conflicts , Changes and Staged . The right side shows the diff of the selected file. Key Action space Stage or unstage the file. a Stage or unstage everything. ↵ Open the diff to stage single lines or hunks. c Commit what is staged. A Amend the last commit. d Discard your edits to the file (asks first, and cannot be undone). e Open the file in your editor. i Add the file to .gitignore . L and B History of the file, and blame. canopy console: Changes Details: Write good commits . While a merge or rebase has conflicts, this tab also shows the conflict panel: see Resolve a merge conflict . History (3) # Every commit, newest first, with a branch graph, the short hash, message, author and age. The commit's changes show on the right; ↵ moves into them and h moves back. / searches. From here you can act on a commit: Key Action space Check out the commit. n Start a new branch at the commit. T Tag the commit. C and t Cherry-pick the commit, and revert it. r Reset the branch to the commit (a menu asks how). i Interactive rebase from the commit. F Fold staged changes into the commit as a fixup. y Copy the hash. canopy console: History Related: Tidy up commits before sharing and Undo a mistake . Branches (4) # Local and remote branches with their ahead and behind counts. ] and [ switch the list between Branches , Tags , Remotes , Worktrees and Submodules . Key Action space or ↵ Check out the branch. n New branch. M and r Merge the selected branch into the current one, and rebase the current one onto it. R and u Rename, and set the upstream. d Delete. canopy console: Branches Details: Branches, merging and rebasing , Tag a version and publish a release and Work on two branches at once . Stash (5) # Work you have put aside. Press S anywhere to stash your changes (untracked files included); here space applies the selected stash, g pops it and d drops it. canopy console: Stash Details: Put work aside with the stash . Workspace (6) # Every repository canopy can find in your workspace folders, with its branch, whether it has changes, and how far ahead or behind it is. ↵ opens one, F fetches all of them, and / filters the list. Set the folders with workspace_dirs in the config . canopy console: Workspace Reflog (7) # Every place HEAD has been: commits, checkouts, resets, merges. It is your saf"},{"url":"wiki/console/daily-workflow.html","title":"Your daily workflow","section":"canopy console","lede":"The loop you'll repeat every day in canopy console: pull, branch, edit, stage, commit, push, open a pull request.","headings":[{"id":"loop","text":"The loop, in one line each"},{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"tips","text":"Tips"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Most days with git follow one loop: get up to date, make a branch, change things, commit in small pieces, push, and open a pull request. This page walks through it once in canopy console, and links to the page that explains each step. The loop assumes your repository is on GitHub. If it is not, skip the pull request steps; everything else still applies. The loop, in one line each # Pull so you start from the latest work. Branch for the change, named after it. Edit your files in your editor. Stage and commit , in small, focused commits. Push the branch. Open a pull request , and watch CI. In canopy console # Start up to date. Run canopy in your repo and press p to pull. Home tells you if you are behind: \"3 new commit(s) on the remote. Press p to pull.\" Make a branch. Press 4 for Branches, then n , name it after the change (spaces become hyphens) and press ↵ . canopy switches to it. See Branches, merging and rebasing . Edit your files in your editor. canopy notices changes on its own; ⌃R refreshes right away. Review and stage. On Changes ( 2 ), press space to stage a file, or ↵ to open the diff and stage only some lines. Commit. Press c , write a summary, press ↵ . Repeat steps 3 to 5 as you go. See Write good commits . Push. Press P . The first push of a new branch also sets it to track the remote. If canopy says the branch has diverged, see Sync with a remote . Open a pull request. Press 8 for Pull requests, then n . The branch must be pushed first. See Working with GitHub . Watch CI. Press 0 for Actions. Home's GitHub card shows check results too. canopy console: Home Home's Next steps list suggests what to do from wherever you are, with the key for each. Teach mode is on by default, so the footer shows the git command behind every step; T toggles it. What git runs # $ git pull $ git switch -c fix-login-timeout $ git add -- src/login.rs $ git commit -m \"Raise the login timeout to 30 seconds\" $ git push --set-upstream origin fix-login-timeout $ gh pr create --title \"Raise the login timeout\" --body \"...\" --base main The pull request is made by the GitHub CLI, gh , which canopy uses for everything on GitHub. Tips # Need to switch tasks but not ready to commit? Put the work aside with the stash. See Put work aside with the stash . Committed too early? Amend the last commit. See Fix your last commit . Commit often and small. Small commits are easier to review, and easier to undo. If something goes wrong # Pull stops with a conflict. Follow Resolve a merge conflict . You worked on main by mistake. Make a branch from where you are ( 4 then n ) before you push; your commits come with you. Then move main back with Undo a mistake . You committed the wrong thing. Press z to undo it. canopy asks first, and your changes come back as staged changes. Push is refused. The remote has moved. See Sync with a remote . New PR says to push first. A pull request needs the branch on GitHub. Press P , then try again. Related pages # Git in ten minutes Write good commits Branches, merging and rebasing Sync with a remote Working with GitHub"},{"url":"wiki/console/commits.html","title":"Write good commits","section":"canopy console","lede":"Small, focused commits with clear messages, and how to stage exactly the lines that belong together.","headings":[{"id":"message","text":"Writing a good message"},{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"amend-pointer","text":"Fixing the last commit"},{"id":"teach","text":"Teach mode"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A commit saves a snapshot of what you have staged , together with a message saying why. This page covers writing the message, choosing exactly what goes in, and where to fix a commit you have just made. If the words working tree, staging area and commit are new, read Git in ten minutes first. Writing a good message # Summary: one short line, about 50 characters. Say what the change does: \"Ignore case when counting words\". Description (optional): why you did it, or what a reader might miss. canopy leaves the blank line between the two for you. Say why , not just what . \"Fix crash on an empty file\" beats \"update main.rs\". Only commit related changes together. If a summary needs the word \"and\", you probably have two commits. Staging single lines or hunks (below) is how you split them. In canopy console # Press 2 for the Changes tab. Move with j and k . Press space on a file to stage or unstage it, or a to stage everything. For part of a file, press ↵ to open its diff. Move to a line and press space to stage or unstage it. Press v to select a range first, or ↵ to stage the whole hunk. n and N jump between hunks, and s switches to a split view. h goes back to the list. Press c to open the commit box. ( ⌃S opens it too, and submits it once it is open.) Type a summary. A counter beside it shows the length: green up to 50, \"keep it short\" up to 72, then \"too long for a summary\". Press tab to move to the description. Press ↵ on the summary (or ⌃S ) to commit. esc cancels without committing. canopy console: Changes If nothing is staged, the box says \"Nothing staged: all changes will be staged and committed (git add --all).\" Otherwise it says it commits what is staged and leaves the rest in your working tree. With a clean tree, c says \"Nothing to commit — working tree clean\". Fixing the last commit # Press A on the Changes tab. The box rewrites the last commit with the message you give it and anything you have staged. See Fix your last commit for the full walkthrough. Teach mode # Teach mode is on by default: the footer shows the git command behind each action, such as the git commit you just ran. Press T to hide or show it. What git runs # Staging and committing map to a few plain commands. Line and hunk staging builds a small patch and applies it to the index only, so your files are untouched. $ git add -- src/main.rs $ git add --all $ git restore --staged -- src/main.rs $ git apply --cached --unidiff-zero --whitespace=nowarn - $ git commit -m \"Ignore case when counting words\" $ git commit -m \"Ignore case when counting words\" --amend If something goes wrong # \"Nothing to commit\". There are no changes, or you have not saved your files. Save in your editor and try again. You committed too much or too little. If you have not pushed, amend it ( Fix your last commit ) or press z to undo it. Your changes stay in your files. You committed a file that should be ignored. See Ignore files with .gitignore . Git says it does not know who you are. Set user.name and user.email with git config --global (press ! to run a git command from inside canopy), then commit again. A commit is already pushed. Do not rewrite it. Make a new commit that fixes it, or see Undo a mistake . Related pages # Git in ten minutes Fix your last commit Undo a mistake Ignore files with .gitignore Tidy up commits before sharing"},{"url":"wiki/console/branches.html","title":"Branches, merging and rebasing","section":"canopy console","lede":"What branches are for, the two ways to combine them, and what to do when they collide.","headings":[{"id":"merge-rebase","text":"Merge or rebase?"},{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A branch is a movable name for a commit. When you commit, the branch you are on moves forward. That is all it is, which is why branches are cheap: make one for every change, and main stays working while you experiment. This page covers making, switching, renaming and deleting branches, bringing two branches together with a merge or a rebase, and what to do when they collide. Merge or rebase? # Both bring one branch's commits into another. They differ in the history they leave. Merge Rebase What it does Joins the two lines with a merge commit that has two parents. Replays your commits on top of the other branch, as if you had started from its latest commit. History Keeps what really happened, including the fork. A straight line, no merge commit. Your commits Unchanged. Rewritten: they get new hashes. Best for Bringing a finished branch into main , or any commits others already have. Tidying your own unshared branch before you open a pull request. The one rule. Never rebase commits other people have already pulled. Rebasing rewrites them, and their copies will no longer match yours. In canopy console # Press 4 for the Branches tab. ] and [ switch between branches, tags, remotes, worktrees and submodules. Create: press n , type a name (spaces become hyphens) and press ↵ . It is created from where you are now (HEAD) and checked out. On the History tab ( 3 ), n starts a branch at the selected commit instead. Switch: select a branch and press space or ↵ . Merge: check out the branch that should receive the changes, select the branch to bring in and press M . It runs at once, without asking. Rebase: check out your branch, select the branch you want to build on and press r . Also runs at once. Rename: select a local branch, press R , edit the name, press ↵ . Upstream: press u ; the box is prefilled with origin/<branch> . Pushing a new branch with P also sets it. Delete: press d . A menu offers Delete (only if fully merged) and Force delete (even with unmerged commits); the letters in the menu are d and D . You cannot delete the branch you are on. Deleting a remote branch asks first, because it removes it for everyone. canopy console: Branches Merge and rebase do not ask first. Press z straight afterwards if it was not what you meant. What git runs # $ git switch -c feature/my-idea $ git switch main $ git merge --no-edit feature/my-idea $ git rebase main $ git branch -m old-name new-name $ git branch --set-upstream-to origin/feature/my-idea $ git branch -d feature/my-idea $ git branch -D feature/my-idea $ git push origin --delete feature/my-idea -d refuses to delete unmerged work; -D does not. If something goes wrong # The merge or rebase stops and Changes shows a conflict. Both branches changed the same lines. Follow Resolve a merge conflict , or press X on Changes to abort and go back to where you were. Git refuses to switch branches. You have changes it would overwrite. Commit them, or stash them with the stash , and switch again. Git refuses to delete a branch. It has commits that are not merged anywhere. Merge it first, or force delete only if you are sure you do not need them. You deleted a branch you needed. Its commits are still in the reflog for a while. See Undo a mistake . You merged or rebased the wrong way. Press z to undo it. After a rebase, push is refused. Your commits were rewritten, so the branch has diverged from the remote. See Sync with a remote . Related pages # Resolve a merge conflict Sync with a remote Work on two branches at once Tidy up commits before sharing Undo a mistake"},{"url":"wiki/console/syncing.html","title":"Sync with a remote","section":"canopy console","lede":"Fetch, pull and push, what \"ahead\" and \"behind\" mean, and what to do when your branch and the remote have both moved on.","headings":[{"id":"actions","text":"Fetch, pull and push"},{"id":"diverged","text":"When your branch has diverged"},{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Your repository lives on your computer. A remote (usually called origin ) is another copy, for example on GitHub. Three actions move commits between the two: fetch looks, pull brings other people's commits to you, and push sends yours. Home shows where you stand next to the branch's upstream. ↑1 means one commit only you have (ahead). ↓2 means two commits only the remote has (behind). in sync means neither. Fetch, pull and push # Action What it does Changes your files? Fetch Downloads what is new on every remote, so the ahead and behind counts are up to date. No. Pull Fetches, then brings the remote's new commits into your current branch. Yes: new commits are added, and a merge or rebase may happen. Push Sends your new commits to the remote. No. It changes the remote. Fetch is always safe, so do it whenever you like. Pull before you push if the remote may have moved. When your branch has diverged # A branch has diverged when you and the remote both have commits the other lacks, for example 1 ahead and 2 behind. Git will not simply push, because that would overwrite the remote's commits. You have three choices: Choice What happens Use it when Pull with merge Creates a merge commit joining the two lines. You want to keep history exactly as it happened. Pull with rebase Replays your commits on top of the remote's, then you push again. You want a straight line of history. Your commits get new hashes. Force push Replaces the remote branch with yours. The remote-only commits are lost from that branch. You rewrote your own commits on purpose and nobody else has built on them. Force push is the dangerous one. canopy uses --force-with-lease , which refuses if someone pushed since your last fetch. That protects their work, but it still discards whatever is on the remote branch that you have not seen. When unsure, choose a pull. Both pulls can stop on a conflict; see Resolve a merge conflict . In canopy console # Press f to fetch from all remotes. Branches deleted on the remote are pruned from your list. Press p to pull. Git's own configuration decides between merge and rebase ( pull.rebase ). With no upstream, canopy tells you to push first. Press P to push. If the branch has no upstream, canopy pushes with --set-upstream to origin (or your first remote). If you are behind, P says \"Behind by N — pull first (p)\". If there is nothing to send it says \"Up to date — nothing to push\". If the branch has diverged, P opens \"Branch has diverged: N ahead, M behind\" with three choices: r pull with rebase, m pull with merge, f force push (with lease). Press esc to cancel. The force push is marked as dangerous in the menu. Home warns you too: \"Your branch has diverged (N ahead, M behind). P shows options.\" canopy console: Home Teach mode ( T ) shows each command in the footer as it runs. Press z to undo a pull. What git runs # canopy adds --progress so it can show progress, which is left out below. $ git fetch --prune --all $ git pull $ git pull --rebase $ git pull --no-rebase $ git push origin main $ git push --set-upstream origin main $ git push --force-with-lease origin main The p key runs plain git pull ; the diverged menu adds --rebase or --no-rebase . --force-with-lease overwrites the remote only if it is still where you last saw it. If something goes wrong # \"This branch has no upstream to pull from — push it first (P)\". Push first. The push creates the branch on the remote and sets the upstream. \"No remote configured\". Add one with git remote add origin <url> (press ! to run a raw git command), or set the repository up with Start a new repository . You are on a detached HEAD. There is no branch to push. Press n on the History tab to create a branch first. Pull fails saying the branches have diverged and you must choose merge or rebase. Git has no pull.rebase setting to fall back on. Press P instead: its menu lets you pick. A pull stops on a conflict. Follow Resolve a merge conflict , or press X on Changes to abort the merge or rebase. Push is"},{"url":"wiki/console/github-flow.html","title":"Working with GitHub","section":"canopy console","lede":"Connect canopy console to GitHub, then handle pull requests, CI runs and notifications without opening a browser.","headings":[{"id":"connect","text":"Connect to GitHub"},{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"more","text":"Issues, releases and notifications"},{"id":"git","text":"What runs underneath"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"canopy console talks to GitHub through the GitHub CLI ( gh ). Once you are logged in, pull requests, issues, CI runs, releases and notifications each get a tab, and you never need a browser for the everyday loop. This page covers the whole loop: connect, open a pull request, check CI, merge. For the details of reviewing and CI, see the two recipes it links to. Connect to GitHub # Run gh auth login once in any terminal and follow the prompts. canopy checks with gh auth status ; if you are not logged in, the GitHub tabs tell you so. Nothing else is stored by canopy. If the folder is not on GitHub yet, see Start a new repository . In canopy console # Push your branch. Press P . A pull request needs a branch that is not the default branch and that has an upstream; otherwise canopy says \"Push this branch first (P), then open the pull request\". Open the PR. Press 8 for the PRs tab, then n . The form is headed \"New pull request: your-branch → base\". Fill in the title and description, then submit. Watch CI. Press 0 for Actions . Runs show as passing, failing or in progress. f filters them. If a run failed: select it and press e for details or L for the full log. R re-runs only the failed jobs. A asks your AI assistant to fix it. See Fix a failing CI run . Review. On the PRs tab, f cycles the filter, ↵ opens a PR and D toggles its diff. r opens the review menu: a approve, c comment, x request changes. C adds a plain comment. space checks the PR's branch out locally. See Review a pull request . Merge. Press M : m merge commit, s squash, r rebase. If checks are failing, the menu title says \"! checks are failing\". Then press p on your default branch to bring the merge down. canopy console: PRs o opens the selected PR, issue or run in your browser if you want the full page. canopy console: Actions Issues, releases and notifications # Issues ( 9 ): n opens a new one, C comments, X closes or reopens. Releases: on the Actions tab, ] switches to Releases, where n writes a new one. See Tag a version and publish a release . Notifications ( ⌥N ): ↵ opens one in the browser, m marks it read, M marks all read, u switches between unread and all. Every key for these tabs is in Keyboard shortcuts . What runs underneath # Each action is one gh command; canopy only fills in the details. $ gh auth status $ gh pr list $ gh pr create --title \"Add word counter\" --body \"...\" --base main $ gh pr checkout 42 $ gh pr review 42 --approve $ gh pr merge 42 --squash --delete-branch $ gh issue create --title \"Crash on empty file\" $ gh run list $ gh run rerun 123456 --failed $ gh release create v1.2.0 If something goes wrong # The GitHub tabs say you are not logged in. Run gh auth login in a terminal, then press ⌃R to refresh. \"gh\" is not found. The GitHub CLI is not installed. Install it from cli.github.com, then reopen canopy. You cannot open a PR. You are on the default branch, or the branch has no upstream. Make a branch ( Branches, merging and rebasing ) and push it with P . Push is denied. Your GitHub account lacks write access to that repository. Fork it, or ask the owner. You merged the wrong way. A merge on GitHub cannot be undone from canopy. Revert the merge commit with a new commit, or see Undo a mistake . The PR list is empty but you expect one. Press f to change the filter: it may be showing only one kind. Related pages # Review a pull request Fix a failing CI run Sync with a remote Tag a version and publish a release Start a new repository"},{"url":"wiki/console/fix-last-commit.html","title":"Fix your last commit","section":"canopy console","lede":"A typo in the message, a forgotten file, or a fix that belongs in an older commit.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"amend","text":"The message or a forgotten file (the last commit)"},{"id":"fixup","text":"A change that belongs in an older commit"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Everyone makes a small mistake in a commit: a typo in the message, a file you forgot to add, or a change that belongs in a commit from a few steps back. Git lets you fix all three, and canopy console keeps each fix to a few keys. Fixing a commit really replaces it with a new one (it gets a new id). That is harmless while the commit is only on your computer. If you have already pushed it, read If something goes wrong first. In canopy console # The message or a forgotten file (the last commit) # To add a forgotten file, open Changes ( 2 ), select the file and press space to stage it. To fix only the message, stage nothing. Press A on Changes. A commit box opens with the last message filled in. Edit it if you want. Press ↵ on the summary line (or ⌃S from anywhere in the box) to send it. The last commit is replaced with one that has your new message and everything you staged. Unlike a normal commit, amend never stages everything for you. It uses only what you staged. canopy console: Changes A change that belongs in an older commit # Make the fix and stage it on Changes ( 2 , then space ). Open History ( 3 ), select the commit the fix belongs to and press F . canopy adds a new commit called fixup! … that holds your fix. With that same older commit still selected, press i to start an interactive rebase. The fixup! commit is the last row of the list. Move it up with Shift+K until it sits directly under the commit it fixes, press f to mark it fixup , then ↵ to run. The fix is folded into the older commit and the extra commit disappears. Squash and fixup fold upwards. A row marked fixup or squash merges into the row above it in the box, which is why the fixup! commit has to sit right under the commit it belongs to. The first row can't be either. What git runs # These are the commands canopy console runs for the two fixes: $ git commit -m \"the new message\" --amend $ git commit --fixup=<commit> $ git -c sequence.editor=… -c core.editor=true rebase -i --autostash <parent of that commit> --amend replaces the last commit instead of adding one. --autostash puts uncommitted work aside during the rebase and brings it back afterwards. If something goes wrong # You amended and it was the wrong thing. Press z : the branch moves back to the commit as it was before, with your changes staged. See Undo a mistake . You already pushed the commit. An amended commit is a different commit, so the next push is refused. canopy console then offers Force push (with lease), which replaces the remote copy with yours. Only do that on a branch nobody else is using; otherwise leave the old commit alone and make a new commit with the fix. \"Stage the changes you want to fold into this commit first\". F needs something staged. Stage the fix on Changes and try again. \"No commits to amend yet\". The repository has no commit. Make a normal commit with c first. \"That range contains merge commits\". The interactive rebase won't run over merge commits, because it would flatten them. Select a commit that comes after the last merge. \"Clear the filter (esc) before rebasing\". You are searching History. Press esc first. The rebase stops with a conflict. See Resolve a merge conflict : resolve it and continue, or abort to go back to how it was. Related pages # Undo a mistake Tidy up commits before sharing Write good commits Sync with a remote"},{"url":"wiki/console/undo.html","title":"Undo a mistake","section":"canopy console","lede":"Take back a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back work you thought was lost.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"undo","text":"Undo the last action"},{"id":"reset","text":"Go back further: reset"},{"id":"revert","text":"Undo a commit you already shared: revert"},{"id":"discard","text":"Throw away uncommitted edits on purpose"},{"id":"reflog","text":"Get back something that seems gone"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Git rarely loses anything. A commit, a merge, a reset or a checkout can nearly always be taken back, because git remembers where your branch used to point. The one thing it cannot bring back is edits you never committed and then threw away. The quick way is z . It looks at the last thing that moved your branch, tells you exactly what it will do, and only does it when you agree. For anything older, use a reset or the Reflog. In canopy console # Undo the last action # Press z from any screen. A box Undo last action? shows the last action and what will happen (or Undo checkout? if you last switched branch). Press ↵ to confirm, or esc to cancel. Nothing changes until you confirm. What z does depends on what you did last: Last action Undo does this A commit (or an amend) Moves the branch back one commit. The commit's changes come back as staged changes, ready to fix and commit again. A checkout (switching branch) Switches back to the branch you came from. A merge, cherry-pick, revert, reset or rebase Moves the branch back to where it was before, and keeps your uncommitted changes. A whole rebase counts as one action. Undo is built from git's reflog, so it can't take back a push, a discard or a dropped stash. Go back further: reset # Open History ( 3 ) and select the commit you want to go back to. Press r . The box Reset current branch to … asks how to treat the changes in the commits that leave the branch. Mixed is highlighted. Press s for Soft (their changes stay staged), m for Mixed (their changes stay, unstaged) or h for Hard (their changes and your uncommitted work are thrown away). You can also move with j and k and press ↵ . After a Hard reset, z brings the commits back, but uncommitted edits are gone for good. Undo a commit you already shared: revert # If the commit is on the remote, don't rewrite history. Select it on History and press t . That adds a new commit that undoes its changes and keeps the old one in the history, so nobody else's copy breaks. Throw away uncommitted edits on purpose # On Changes ( 2 ), select an unstaged file and press d . canopy shows what will be lost and asks first: a new file is deleted, and for a changed file your unstaged edits are thrown away. The box says This cannot be undone , and it is right, because git never saved those edits. If the file is staged, unstage it first with space . Get back something that seems gone # The Reflog screen lists everywhere your branch has been, including commits no branch points to any more, such as ones you reset away or an old version of a commit you amended. Open Reflog ( 7 ) and find the entry from before the mistake. Press ↵ to look at that commit. Press r and choose m (Mixed) to move your branch there and keep your files as they are, or h (Hard) to make your files match it too. canopy console: Reflog What git runs # Undo and the reset keys are git commands on your branch. Undo uses --soft after a commit and --keep otherwise, and falls back to --mixed if --keep refuses because your edits touch files that differ. $ git reset --soft <previous commit> $ git reset --keep <previous commit> $ git switch <branch you came from> $ git reset --soft|--mixed|--hard <commit> $ git revert --no-edit <commit> $ git restore --worktree -- <file> $ git clean -f -d -- <new file> The last two are Discard: restore for a changed file, clean to delete a new one. The difference between reset and revert: reset moves the branch back, so the later commits leave it. revert keeps them and adds a commit that cancels one out. If something goes wrong # \"Nothing to undo\". The reflog has no earlier step for this repository. Use reset instead. You chose Hard by mistake. The commits come back (press z , or find the old one in Reflog and reset to it), but uncommitted edits are gone for good. You pushed already. Undo and reset only change your copy. If others may have pulled the commit, use revert. If you reset a pushed branch, pushing the result needs a force push, which replaces the remote "},{"url":"wiki/console/conflicts.html","title":"Resolve a merge conflict","section":"canopy console","lede":"When a merge, rebase or cherry-pick stops because both sides changed the same lines.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A conflict happens when two branches changed the same lines of the same file and git can't tell which version to keep. It stops in the middle of a merge, rebase or cherry-pick and asks you. Nothing is broken and nothing is lost; you decide, and then git carries on. In the conflicted file, git marks the two versions like this: <<<<<<< HEAD the version on your current branch (ours) ======= the version from the other branch (theirs) >>>>>>> other-branch Resolving means ending up with the text you want, with all three marker lines gone. The two names matter for the ours and theirs keys below: in a merge , \"ours\" is the branch you are on and \"theirs\" is the one you are merging in. In a rebase they are the other way round: \"ours\" is the branch you are rebasing onto, and \"theirs\" is your own commit being replayed. In canopy console # When an operation stops on conflicts, Changes shows that a merge (or rebase, cherry-pick, revert) is in progress and lists the conflicted files under their own heading. canopy console resolves them one conflict at a time. Open Changes ( 2 ). Select a conflicted file and press ↵ to open the conflict panel, which shows each conflict with both sides. Press j and k to move between conflicts. For the one you are on, press o to keep ours, t to take theirs, or b to keep both (ours, then theirs). Changed your mind? Press u to put the conflict markers back in the file. Press e to edit the file by hand in your editor. When the last conflict in a file is settled, canopy stages the file for you. Press esc to go back to the list and open the next file. When no conflicted files are left, press C to continue the merge (or rebase, cherry-pick or revert). To give up, press X and confirm. Everything goes back to how it was before it started. On the file list you can also settle a whole file at once: o keeps ours for the entire file, t takes theirs, and space marks a file you edited yourself as resolved (canopy refuses if markers are still in it). canopy console: Conflicts What git runs # $ git checkout --ours -- <file> # or --theirs $ git add <file> $ git checkout -m -- <file> # put the markers back $ git -c core.editor=true merge --continue $ git merge --abort Choosing ours or theirs for a whole file is the first two lines. Choosing a single conflict rewrites the file itself, then stages it once none are left. For a rebase, cherry-pick or revert, canopy console runs that command's --continue and --abort instead of merge . If something goes wrong # \"Still has conflict markers\". canopy console won't mark a file resolved while <<<<<<< or >>>>>>> lines are in it. Edit them out ( e ), or choose a side with o or t . \"Select a conflicted file first\". The cursor is on a file that isn't under the conflicts heading. Move to a conflicted file and try again. Nothing happens when you press C . A file may still be listed as conflicted. Settle it, and C continues. Ours kept the wrong side. During a rebase, \"ours\" and \"theirs\" swap (see the top of this page). Press u to restore the markers and choose again. It went badly and you want to start over. Press X to abort. Nothing is lost. After the merge is finished, you can still take it back: see Undo a mistake . You'd like fewer conflicts. Pull often and keep branches short. See Branches, merging and rebasing . Related pages # Branches, merging and rebasing Sync with a remote Tidy up commits before sharing Undo a mistake"},{"url":"wiki/console/stash.html","title":"Put work aside with the stash","section":"canopy console","lede":"Save half-done changes without committing them, switch to something else, and bring them back later.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"put-aside","text":"Put changes aside"},{"id":"bring-back","text":"Bring it back"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"The stash is a shelf for half-done work. You have changes you are not ready to commit, but you need a clean folder right now: to switch branches, pull, or fix something urgent. Stash them, do the other thing, then bring them back. A stash is stored inside your repository (not sent to GitHub), and you can keep several. Each one is named stash@{0} , stash@{1} and so on, newest first. In canopy console # Put changes aside # Press S from any screen. Type a message if you want one, so you recognise the stash later. New (untracked) files are always included. Press ↵ . Your files go back to the last commit and the stash is saved. (With nothing changed, canopy says Nothing to stash .) Bring it back # Open the Stash screen ( 5 ). Move with j and k ; ↵ shows what is inside. Press g to pop it (bring the changes back and remove the stash), or space to apply it (bring them back and keep the stash, handy to try the same changes on another branch). Press d to drop a stash you don't need any more. canopy shows Drop stash? and asks first. canopy console: Stash What git runs # The stash commands are plain git. --include-untracked is what puts brand-new files aside too. $ git stash push --include-untracked -m \"half-done refactor\" $ git stash apply stash@{0} $ git stash pop stash@{0} $ git stash drop stash@{0} If something goes wrong # Apply or pop stops with an error about local changes. Git won't bring a stash back over files you have changed since. Commit those changes or stash them first, then try again. Nothing was lost; the stash is still in the list. Pop leaves conflicts. The stash and your current files changed the same lines. The stash is kept, so nothing is lost. Fix the files on Changes and drop the stash when you are done. See Resolve a merge conflict . The stash is on the wrong branch. Stashes are not tied to a branch. Switch to the right branch and apply it there. You dropped one by mistake. z doesn't cover dropped stashes. Git usually still holds the content for a while, but finding it takes advanced git commands, so read the name in the confirmation box before you press ↵ . You popped and want it back on the shelf. Stash again with S . Undo a mistake covers commits and resets, not stashes. Related pages # Work on two branches at once (switch without stashing) Branches, merging and rebasing Resolve a merge conflict Undo a mistake"},{"url":"wiki/console/ignore-files.html","title":"Ignore files with .gitignore","section":"canopy console","lede":"Keep build output, secrets and editor clutter out of git, and stop tracking a file you committed by mistake.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"patterns","text":"Patterns you'll use"},{"id":"untrack","text":"Stop tracking a file you already committed"},{"id":"global","text":"Ignore files on every project"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Some files should never be in git: build output, downloaded packages such as node_modules , secrets like .env , and clutter your computer makes ( .DS_Store ). A file named .gitignore at the top of your project lists what git should leave alone. Ignored files stop showing up in your changes and can't be staged by accident. Ignoring only works for files git is not tracking yet. If you already committed a file, ignoring it is not enough: see Stop tracking a file you already committed . In canopy console # Open Changes ( 2 ) and select the file you want git to leave alone. Press i . canopy adds the file's path to the .gitignore in the top folder of the repository (creating it if needed) and shows Ignored … . The file drops out of the list and .gitignore appears as a change. Stage and commit it ( space , then c ) so your teammates get the same list. i adds one exact path (for example /build/output.log ). To ignore a whole kind of file, such as every *.log , edit .gitignore yourself: select it on Changes and press e , or use any editor, with the patterns below. canopy console: Changes A new repository can also start with a ready-made list (Rust, Node, Python or macOS); the setup asks which. See Start a new repository . What git runs # Adding to .gitignore is a text edit, not a git command. i appends the path to the file, and the command log records it like this: $ echo '/path/to/file' >> .gitignore Git reads .gitignore on its own the next time it looks at your files. Patterns you'll use # Line Ignores .env Any file called .env , in any folder /target Only target at the top of the project (a leading / means \"from the top\") node_modules/ Any folder called node_modules (a trailing / means folder) *.log Every file ending in .log !keep.log An exception: don't ignore this one # a note A comment Stop tracking a file you already committed # If a file was committed before you ignored it, git keeps tracking it, and .gitignore has no effect on it. To make git forget the file without deleting it from your disk: Add the file to .gitignore as above. Press ! to open the git command box and run rm --cached path/to/file (use rm -r --cached folder for a folder). canopy adds git in front for you. The deletion shows up on Changes as a staged change. Commit it. From then on the file stays on your disk but out of git. Secrets stay in history. This stops tracking the file from now on, but earlier commits still contain it. If it held a password or a key, treat that secret as leaked and change it. Ignore files on every project # For clutter that comes from your own computer and editor, rather than the project, a global ignore file saves editing every repository. Run this once, in ! or any terminal: $ git config --global core.excludesFile ~/.gitignore_global Then list patterns such as .DS_Store in ~/.gitignore_global . This is plain git, not something canopy sets up for you. If something goes wrong # The file still shows up after you ignored it. It is already tracked. Follow Stop tracking a file you already committed . A pattern ignores too much. Make it more specific (start with / to anchor it at the top of the project) or add an exception with ! . Then look at Changes to see what came back. You need to commit an ignored file once. canopy console can't stage an ignored file. Remove or narrow its pattern first, or run add -f file in the ! box. You pressed i on the wrong file. z does not cover edits to .gitignore . Select .gitignore on Changes, press e to open it, and delete the last line. Related pages # Start a new repository Write good commits Undo a mistake Troubleshooting"},{"url":"wiki/console/clean-history.html","title":"Tidy up commits before sharing","section":"canopy console","lede":"Squash, reorder, reword and drop commits with an interactive rebase.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"paused","text":"If it stops"},{"id":"squash-all","text":"Squash everything into one commit"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"While you work you make commits like \"wip\", \"fix typo\" and \"oops, forgot this\". Before you share a branch (push it or open a pull request), you can tidy them: squash several into one, drop ones you don't want, reorder them. Git calls this an interactive rebase . It replays your commits one at a time, and for each one you choose what happens. Only tidy commits nobody else has. Tidying replaces your commits with new ones. That is safe while they are only on your computer or on a branch only you use. If others have pulled them, rewriting them causes trouble, so leave shared history alone. The five choices for each commit are: pick (keep it as it is), squash (merge it into the commit before it and combine both messages), fixup (merge it into the commit before it and throw away its message), edit (stop there so you can change it) and drop (delete it). In canopy console # Open History ( 3 ) and select the oldest commit you want to change. The list shows newest first. The rebase covers the selected commit and every commit above it, up to your latest. Press i . An Interactive rebase box lists those commits, oldest first, each marked pick . Choose what to do with each commit. Move with j and k , then press p (pick), s (squash), f (fixup), e (edit) or d (drop; the message is crossed out). Squash and fixup fold a commit into the one above it in the box , so they aren't allowed on the first row. To reorder, press Shift+J or Shift+K to move the selected commit down or up. Press ↵ to run it, or esc to cancel without changing anything. canopy console: History Changing a commit's message. The box has no reword choice. Mark that commit edit and run it. The rebase stops there. Press A on Changes, fix the message, send it, then press C to continue. If it stops # An edit commit, or a conflict, pauses the rebase. Changes shows that a rebase is in progress. Press C to continue when you are ready, or X to abort and get everything back the way it was. Squash everything into one commit # If you only want one commit out of several, a reset is quicker than a rebase. On History, select the commit just before the ones you want to combine, press r and choose s (Soft). The commits leave the branch and all their changes are staged together. Then press c on Changes and write one clear message. See Undo a mistake for what the reset choices mean. What git runs # canopy writes your choices into the list git would normally open in an editor, then runs the rebase without opening one: $ git -c sequence.editor=\"cp <your list>\" -c core.editor=true rebase -i --autostash <parent of the oldest commit> $ git -c core.editor=true rebase --continue $ git rebase --abort core.editor=true means a squash keeps both messages without stopping to ask. --autostash sets uncommitted work aside during the rebase and puts it back after. If something goes wrong # \"That range contains merge commits\". An interactive rebase would flatten them, so canopy refuses. Select a commit that comes after the last merge. \"Can't rebase the root commit from here\". Select a commit that has a parent. \"Clear the filter (esc) before rebasing\". You are searching History. Press esc first, so the list is your real history. The rebase stops with a conflict. Two of your commits changed the same lines in a way git can't combine. See Resolve a merge conflict , then continue, or abort. You didn't like the result. Press z : a whole rebase counts as one action, and Undo moves the branch back to before it started. See Undo a mistake . You already pushed these commits. After tidying, the branch and its remote copy have diverged, so the push box offers Force push (with lease). Use it only if nobody else builds on this branch. Related pages # Fix your last commit Undo a mistake Resolve a merge conflict Branches, merging and rebasing"},{"url":"wiki/console/find-a-bug.html","title":"Find the commit that broke something","section":"canopy console","lede":"Bisect to the commit that introduced a bug, then use blame and file history to understand it.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"bisect","text":"Bisect: let git do the halving"},{"id":"blame","text":"Blame: who changed this line"},{"id":"file-history","text":"File history"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Something worked last week and doesn't now. Somewhere in between, a commit broke it. You can find that commit without reading every change: pick a commit where things still worked, and git narrows the range by half each time you test one. That search is called bisect . Blame and file history help once you have found the commit: they show who changed a line, and what happened to a file over time. In canopy console # Bisect: let git do the halving # Commit or stash your changes. canopy stops with a message if there are uncommitted changes to tracked files, because bisect checks out other commits. Press 3 for History and select a commit where things still worked. It has to be older than the newest commit. Press b . canopy asks Start bisect? and shows the good commit you picked and that HEAD, what you have now, counts as bad. Confirm with ↵ . Git checks out a commit halfway between. Build it, run it, do whatever shows the bug. Press b again and choose Good , Bad or Skip (skip if you cannot test that commit). The prompt shows about how many steps are left. When git finds it, canopy says Found it and names the commit. Choose Finish and show it to stop bisecting and jump to that commit in History. To give up at any point, choose Stop bisecting . That takes you back to where you started. Blame: who changed this line # On Changes ( 2 ), or on a file in a commit's diff, press B . Each line shows the commit that last changed it. ↵ jumps to that commit. B again shows the file as it was just before that change, so you can keep digging back. canopy console: Blame File history # Press L on a file to see only the commits that touched it, following renames. esc goes back to the full history. What git runs # Bisect starts with your current commit as bad and the commit you picked as good. Blame and file history are read-only. $ git bisect start HEAD <good commit> $ git bisect good $ git bisect bad $ git bisect skip $ git bisect reset $ git blame --porcelain -- <file> $ git log --follow -- <file> The log line also carries canopy's own format and paging options. If something goes wrong # \"Commit or stash your changes first.\" Bisect will not start with uncommitted changes to tracked files. Commit them, or stash them and bring them back afterwards ( stash ). \"Select an older commit where things still worked.\" You selected the newest commit. Select an older one, then press b . You are in the middle of a bisect and want out. Press b and choose Stop bisecting . It runs git bisect reset and returns you to your branch. Blame shows \"not committed\". Those lines are edits you have not committed yet, so no commit owns them. You checked out an old commit while testing and lost track of your branch. Go to Branches ( 4 ), select your branch and press space . Work done on a checkout with no branch is not on any branch; see Undo a mistake for finding it again. Related pages # Put work aside with the stash Undo a mistake Branches, merging and rebasing Write good commits"},{"url":"wiki/console/two-branches.html","title":"Work on two branches at once","section":"canopy console","lede":"Check out a second branch in its own folder with a worktree, so you never have to stash to switch.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Switching branches with half-done work in your folder means committing it or stashing it first. A worktree avoids that: it is a second folder for the same repository with a different branch checked out. Each folder has its own files and changes, and both share the same history, so a commit made in one shows up in the other. Use a worktree when you want to keep something running or half-finished in one folder (a long build, a dev server, an unfinished refactor) while you fix something urgent on another branch. In canopy console # Press 4 for Branches, then ] until you reach Worktrees . Press n and type a branch name, existing or new. The folder is made next to your repository: acme-app with fix/login becomes acme-app-fix-login . Select the new worktree and press ↵ to open it in canopy, or open the folder in your editor. When you are done, select the worktree and press d . Choose Remove , which only works if the worktree has no uncommitted changes. Force remove deletes the folder and throws those changes away. If you deleted a worktree folder by hand, press x to tidy up the leftover record. Stash instead. For a quick switch you can stash: S puts your changes aside (new files included), and on Stash ( 5 ) g pops them back. See Put work aside with the stash . What git runs # canopy passes a full folder path; <folder> stands for it here. A new branch gets -b . $ git worktree add -b <new branch> <folder> $ git worktree add <folder> <existing branch> $ git worktree list --porcelain $ git worktree remove <folder> $ git worktree remove --force <folder> $ git worktree prune --verbose If something goes wrong # Git will not check out a branch that another folder already has. A branch can only be checked out in one worktree at a time. Use a different branch name, or remove the other worktree first. \"That's the main worktree; it can't be removed.\" The first entry in the list is your original folder. Only the extra ones can be removed. \"canopy has this worktree open; open another one first.\" Open a different worktree (or the original) with ↵ , then remove this one. Remove refuses because of uncommitted changes. Commit or stash them in that folder, or choose Force remove only if you are sure you do not need them. Lost changes? See Undo a mistake for getting work back. Related pages # Put work aside with the stash Branches, merging and rebasing Resolve a merge conflict Undo a mistake"},{"url":"wiki/console/tags-releases.html","title":"Tag a version and publish a release","section":"canopy console","lede":"Mark a commit as v1.2.0, push the tag, and publish a GitHub release with notes.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"tag-a-commit","text":"Tag a commit"},{"id":"delete-a-tag","text":"Delete a tag"},{"id":"create-a-release","text":"Create a release"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A tag is a permanent name for one commit, such as v1.2.0 . It marks \"this is the version we shipped\". A release is a page on GitHub built on a tag, with a title, notes and downloads. The steps are always the same: tag the commit, push the tag, then create the release on GitHub. canopy console does all three. Lightweight or annotated. A lightweight tag is only a name. An annotated tag also stores a message, your name and the date. Either works for a release. If you want a note attached to the tag, write a message. In canopy console # Tag a commit # Press 3 for History, select the commit and press T . Type the tag name, for example v1.2.0 , and press ↵ . This makes a lightweight tag. To tag the commit you have checked out, with a message, press 4 for Branches and ] until you reach Tags , then press n . Type v1.2.0 , or v1.2.0: what changed for an annotated tag. Spaces in the name become - . Press P on the Tags view to push the selected tag. It goes to origin , or to the first remote. If there is no remote you get the message \"No remote to push to\". Delete a tag # On the Tags view, select the tag and press d . Choose Delete locally to remove it from this computer only, or Delete here and on origin (the menu names your remote) to remove it from the remote too. esc cancels. On the Tags view, space or ↵ checks out the tag after a confirmation. This puts you on the tagged commit with no branch, which is fine for looking around but not for new work. Create a release # Push the tag first (see above). If the tag does not exist on GitHub, GitHub creates it for you on the default branch, which may not be the commit you meant. Press 0 for Actions, then ] to switch to Releases . Press n . The suggested tag is the next one: the latest published release with its patch number plus one (v1.0.13 becomes v1.0.14), or v0.1.0 if there is no release yet. Edit it or accept it with ↵ . In the form, the title starts as the tag name. Write notes in the body, or leave the body empty and GitHub generates notes from the merged pull requests. Press ⌃S to publish, or ↵ while the cursor is in the title line. tab moves between the title and the body, and esc cancels. Select a release and press o to open it in your browser. Published straight away. canopy never makes a draft release. The release goes live as soon as you submit, and needs gh to be signed in. You can edit or delete it on GitHub afterwards. What git runs # Tags use plain git. Releases use the GitHub command line, gh . $ git tag v1.2.0 <commit> $ git tag -a v1.2.0 -m \"what changed\" <commit> $ git push origin v1.2.0 $ git tag -d v1.2.0 $ git push origin --delete refs/tags/v1.2.0 $ gh release create v1.2.0 --title \"v1.2.0\" --generate-notes $ gh release create v1.2.0 --title \"v1.2.0\" --notes \"what changed\" The first tag line appears when you give no message, the second when you do. Delete here and on origin runs the git push --delete line first and then git tag -d . Releases use --generate-notes when the notes are empty and --notes otherwise. If something goes wrong # The tag already exists. Tag names are unique. Pick a new name, or delete the old tag first. Moving a tag that others may have downloaded is best avoided. \"No remote to push to\". The repository has no remote. Connect it to GitHub first: see Sync with a remote . You pushed the wrong tag. On the Tags view press d and choose Delete here and on origin , then push the right one. The release points at the wrong commit. That happens when the tag was not pushed before you created the release. Delete the release on GitHub, push the tag, and create it again. Creating a release fails. Check that gh is signed in ( gh auth status ) and that you can write to the repository. You tagged the wrong commit locally. Delete the tag and tag again. If you have not pushed it, nothing else is affected. For other mistakes see Undo a mistake . Related pages # Sync with a remote Working with GitHub Branches, merging and rebasing Undo a mistake"},{"url":"wiki/console/review-a-pr.html","title":"Review a pull request","section":"canopy console","lede":"Read a pull request, comment, and approve or ask for changes.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"merge-choices","text":"Which merge to pick"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A pull request (PR) is a proposal to merge one branch into another. Reviewing one means reading the changes, then telling the author to go ahead (approve), to change something (request changes), or just leaving a comment. canopy console uses GitHub's command line, gh , so you need to be signed in with gh auth login first. Everything you do shows up on GitHub as you. In canopy console # Press 8 for Pull requests. Press f to cycle the list between open, mine, review requested and all. Select a PR and press D to show its diff. Move with j and k . D again hides the diff. To comment on a line, move to it in the diff and press C . Write the comment and press ⌃S . If you press C on the list instead of the diff, it adds a general comment on the PR. To test the code, press space on the PR to check out its branch. Commit or stash your own changes first. Press r to review. Choose Approve , Comment or Request changes . Write your message and press ⌃S . A message is required unless you approve. esc cancels. Press M to merge and choose Merge commit , Squash or Rebase . If checks are failing, the menu title warns \"! checks are failing\". Press X to close a PR without merging, o to open it in your browser, and n to start a new one. The branch is deleted. Merging from canopy console always deletes the PR's branch afterwards. The commits are still in the merged history. canopy console: Pull requests Which merge to pick # Choice What ends up on the main branch Squash One new commit holding all the PR's changes. Keeps history short. Merge commit All the PR's commits, plus a merge commit joining them. Rebase All the PR's commits, replayed one by one on top of the main branch. No merge commit. A merge happens on GitHub, so canopy's own undo ( z ) does not reverse it. If it was a mistake, use the Revert button on the merged PR on GitHub, or see Undo a mistake . What git runs # Pull requests are GitHub's, so canopy runs gh . <n> is the PR number. $ gh pr checkout <n> $ gh pr diff <n> --color never $ gh pr comment <n> --body \"...\" $ gh pr review <n> --approve $ gh pr review <n> --request-changes --body \"...\" $ gh pr review <n> --comment --body \"...\" $ gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch $ gh pr merge <n> --merge --delete-branch $ gh pr merge <n> --rebase --delete-branch A line comment goes through gh api to the pull request's comments endpoint, with the file, the line and which side of the diff it is on. If something goes wrong # Nothing loads, or a gh error appears. Run gh auth status in a terminal. If you are not signed in, run gh auth login . \"Line comments work on a pull request's diff\". You pressed C outside the diff view. Press D on the PR first, then move to a line. \"a review needs a message\". Request changes and Comment reviews need text. Only Approve can go without. Merge is refused. The branch may be out of date, in conflict, or protected by rules that need approvals or passing checks. Fix that on GitHub or in the branch, see Resolve a merge conflict , then merge again. Check out fails because of your local changes. Commit or stash them first, see Put work aside with the stash . Checks are red. See Fix a failing CI run . Related pages # Working with GitHub Fix a failing CI run Resolve a merge conflict Undo a mistake"},{"url":"wiki/console/fix-ci.html","title":"Fix a failing CI run","section":"canopy console","lede":"Find out why CI failed, fix it (or re-run it), and get back to green.","headings":[{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"choose-the-assistant","text":"Choose the assistant"},{"id":"what-the-assistant-gets","text":"What the assistant is given"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"CI (continuous integration) is a set of checks GitHub runs every time you push: tests, a build, a linter. A red cross means one of them failed. The reason is in the run's log, which is long and hard to read. canopy console reads the log for you and shows the failed step first. It can also re-run a failed run, or write a prompt from the failure and open your AI assistant with it. You need gh signed in ( gh auth login ). In canopy console # Press 0 for Actions. Press f to cycle the filter if you cannot see the run you want. Select the failed run. Press e for its details, or L for the full failed log, with the failed jobs first. Press A to fix it with AI. It writes the prompt and opens your assistant in a new terminal tab (a new window if a tab is not possible). Read what the assistant suggests, then make the change. The assistant is told not to commit or push, so those stay under your control. Press R to re-run only the failed jobs, useful when the failure was a flaky test or a network hiccup and you changed nothing. Both A and R only work on failed runs. Press o to open the run in your browser. canopy console: Actions Once you have a fix, commit it with c and push with P . GitHub starts a new run on its own. Choose the assistant # Two settings in config.toml control this. ai picks the assistant: auto (the first of Claude Code and Codex that is installed), claude , codex , off , or your own command, where {prompt} stands for the prompt text. ai_open picks where it opens: tab (the default), window , or here , which runs it in this terminal and puts canopy aside until it exits. See Configuration . What the assistant is given # The prompt is a file, .git/canopy/fix-ci.md . It lives inside the .git folder, so it is never committed. It holds: the failed job and step, and GitHub's summary of the run, the last 200 lines of the cleaned-up log, instructions: find the cause, make the smallest fix, run the checks, explain what you did, and do not commit or push. The assistant starts with a short message that points at this file. The prompt goes to the assistant you chose, and through it to that service, so do not use this on a repository whose logs must not leave your machine. What git runs # Runs are read with gh . Nothing here changes your repository until you commit yourself. $ gh run view <id> --json jobs $ gh run view <id> $ gh run view <id> --log-failed $ gh run rerun <id> --failed The first three build the failure summary and the prompt, and --log-failed is also what L shows. The last one is the re-run key, R . If something goes wrong # \"No AI assistant set up\". Install Claude Code or Codex, or set ai = \"your command\" in the config. See Configuration . On a Mac, the assistant does not open. canopy needs permission to open Terminal windows. If you said no, allow it in System Settings, Privacy & Security, Automation, then press A again. The prompt file is already saved in .git/canopy/fix-ci.md , so you can also start your assistant yourself and point it there. \"Pick a failed run: A writes a fix-it prompt from its error\". The selected run did not fail. Select a red one. \"Only failed runs can be re-run from here\". Same reason for R . To run a passing run again, do it on GitHub. The re-run fails again. The failure is real. Read the log, fix the code, then push. It passes on your computer and fails on GitHub. The run uses a clean machine. Look for a missing file, a version difference, or a file your .gitignore hides. See Ignore files with .gitignore . You pushed a fix that made things worse. See Undo a mistake . Related pages # Working with GitHub Review a pull request Write good commits Sync with a remote Undo a mistake"},{"url":"wiki/console/new-repo.html","title":"Start a new repository","section":"canopy console","lede":"Turn a folder into a git repository, then put it on GitHub over HTTPS or SSH.","headings":[{"id":"https-or-ssh","text":"HTTPS or SSH"},{"id":"steps","text":"In canopy console"},{"id":"git","text":"What git runs"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"A git repository is a folder that git keeps track of. Starting one is a one-time job: tell git to watch the folder, decide on a branch name and a few starter files, make a first commit, and optionally put a copy on GitHub. Nothing is overwritten. If the folder already has files, canopy leaves them as they are; it only adds a starter file that is missing. HTTPS or SSH # If you connect to a repository that already exists on GitHub, you choose how to reach it. Both work. Kind Address looks like Good to know HTTPS https://github.com/owner/name.git Works everywhere. You sign in through the browser or with a token. gh auth login sets it up. SSH git@github.com:owner/name.git Uses an SSH key. No password prompts once the key is added to your GitHub account. Check it with ssh -T git@github.com . Which one canopy suggests. It follows your gh setting ( gh config get git_protocol ), or picks SSH if this computer already has an SSH key. You can switch later with git remote set-url origin <other address> , or in Branches, Remotes. In canopy console # Go to the folder and run canopy , or run canopy <folder> from anywhere. With no repository there, a menu titled \"No git repository in <folder>\" appears. Choose Create a repository here . The other choices are Show repositories under this folder and Quit . Move with j and k and confirm with ↵ , or press the letter shown next to a choice. Type a branch name (the default is main ) and press ↵ . Then choose a .gitignore template. At \"Start with which files?\" choose A README, and make the first commit , README + MIT license, and the first commit , or Nothing yet: an empty repository . canopy console only offers a README together with the first commit. At \"Connect it to GitHub?\" choose Create it on GitHub (when gh is signed in, then Private or Public), Connect an existing GitHub repository (type owner/name or the address, then pick HTTPS or SSH), or Skip for now . esc cancels a menu. Skipped GitHub? You can add it later. Open Branches ( 4 ), press ] to reach Remotes, and add one there with n . What git runs # The first line makes the repository. The rest depend on your choices. $ git init -b main $ git add --all $ git commit -m \"Initial commit\" $ gh repo create acme-app --private --source=. --remote origin --push $ git remote add origin https://github.com/owner/name.git $ git push --progress --set-upstream origin main The gh repo create line is for \"Create it on GitHub\" ( --public for a public one; --push only when there is a commit). The git remote add and git push lines are for an existing repository. If origin already exists, canopy uses git remote set-url origin instead, so trying again is safe. Very old git (before 2.28) has no -b , so canopy runs git init and then git symbolic-ref HEAD refs/heads/main . If something goes wrong # \"git doesn't know your name and email yet\". The first commit needs an author. Run these once, with your own details, then try again: $ git config --global user.name \"Your Name\" $ git config --global user.email you@example.com \"Added the remote, but the push didn't go through\". The repository probably does not exist on GitHub yet. Create it there (empty, no README), then connect again. Connecting again is safe. No \"Create it on GitHub\" choice. gh is not installed or not signed in. Run gh auth login , or use an existing repository. The push asks for a password or is denied. Check your sign-in for the kind of address you used: gh auth login for HTTPS, an SSH key for SSH. See Sync with a remote . The wrong first commit. If you have not pushed, see Fix your last commit or Undo a mistake . A file you did not want was committed. See Ignore files with .gitignore . Related pages # Git in ten minutes Your daily workflow Sync with a remote Ignore files with .gitignore Working with GitHub"},{"url":"wiki/console/keys.html","title":"Keyboard shortcuts","section":"canopy console","lede":"Every key in canopy console, by screen, generated from the app.","headings":[{"id":"k-global","text":"Everywhere"},{"id":"k-status","text":"Changes"},{"id":"k-diff","text":"Diff panel (after ↵)"},{"id":"k-conflict","text":"Conflict panel"},{"id":"k-log","text":"History"},{"id":"k-branches","text":"Branches"},{"id":"k-tags","text":"Tags"},{"id":"k-remotes","text":"Remotes"},{"id":"k-worktrees","text":"Worktrees"},{"id":"k-submodules","text":"Submodules"},{"id":"k-stash","text":"Stash"},{"id":"k-workspace","text":"Workspace"},{"id":"k-reflog","text":"Reflog"},{"id":"k-pulls","text":"Pull requests"},{"id":"k-issues","text":"Issues"},{"id":"k-runs","text":"Actions"},{"id":"k-releases","text":"Releases"},{"id":"k-notifications","text":"Notifications"}],"text":"Every key canopy console understands, one table per screen. The tables are generated from canopy's own key tables, so they always match the app. Keys on a screen take priority over the \"Everywhere\" keys. Press ? in the app for help, and : to open the command palette. The last column is the action's name. Use it to remap a key in the [keys] table of your config: see remapping keys . Keys are shown the Mac way: ⌃ is Control, ⌥ is Option (Alt on other keyboards), ⇧ is Shift. Option shortcuts work in any terminal, including macOS Terminal's default setup where Option types special characters. ⌃Q and ⌥Q quit from anywhere, even inside a dialog. In canopy itself, Linux shows ctrl-q / alt-q instead; set mac_key_symbols in the config to choose. Everywhere # ? ⌥H f1 help help : ⌃P ⌃K command palette palette q ⌃Q ⌥Q ⌃C quit quit 1 ⌥1 go to home goto_home 2 ⌥2 go to changes goto_changes 3 ⌥3 go to history goto_history 4 ⌥4 go to branches goto_branches 5 ⌥5 go to stash goto_stash 6 ⌥6 go to workspace goto_workspace 7 ⌥7 go to reflog goto_reflog 8 ⌥8 go to pull requests goto_pull_requests 9 ⌥9 go to issues goto_issues 0 ⌥0 go to actions goto_actions tab ⌥→ ⌃→ next tab next_screen ⇧tab ⌥← ⌃← previous tab prev_screen ⌃R ⌥R refresh refresh ⌃L redraw the screen redraw c ⌥C ⌃S commit commit f ⌥F fetch fetch p ⌥P pull pull P ⌥⇧P push push z ⌥Z undo last action undo S ⌥S stash changes stash_push b bisect: find the commit that broke something bisect ! ⌥G run git command raw_git T toggle teach mode toggle_teach ⌃T ⌥T cycle theme cycle_theme ⌥N notifications: your GitHub inbox, from any tab show_notifications k ↑ up up j ↓ down down ⌃U pageup page up page_up ⌃D pagedown page down page_down g home top top G end bottom bottom esc back back Changes # space stage/unstage toggle_stage a stage/unstage all stage_all d discard changes discard ↵ l open enter A amend last commit amend e open in editor open_editor i add to .gitignore ignore o resolve: keep ours take_ours t resolve: take theirs take_theirs C continue merge/rebase continue_op X abort merge/rebase abort_op L history of this file file_history B blame: who changed each line blame Diff panel (after ↵) # space stage/unstage line(s) stage_line ↵ stage/unstage hunk stage_hunk v select range range_select n ] next hunk next_hunk N [ previous hunk prev_hunk s toggle split view toggle_side_by_side h ← back back L history of this file file_history B blame: who changed each line blame C comment on this line of a pull request line_comment Conflict panel # o keep ours for this conflict keep_ours t take theirs for this conflict keep_theirs b keep both (ours, then theirs) keep_both j ↓ n next conflict next_conflict k ↑ N previous conflict prev_conflict u restore conflict markers restore_conflict e open in editor open_editor h ← back back History # ↵ l open enter space checkout commit checkout_commit C cherry-pick cherry_pick t revert commit revert r reset to commit reset_menu i interactive rebase from here rebase_interactive n new branch here branch_from_commit T tag commit tag_commit F fixup staged into commit fixup_commit y copy hash copy_hash / search search Branches # space ↵ checkout checkout n new branch new_branch d delete branch delete_branch M merge into current merge r rebase current onto rebase R rename branch rename_branch u set upstream set_upstream / search search ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view Tags # space ↵ checkout tag checkout_tag n new tag at HEAD new_tag d delete tag delete_tag P push tag push_tag ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view / search search Remotes # f fetch this remote fetch_remote n add remote add_remote d remove remote remove_remote e change remote URL edit_remote_url R rename remote rename_remote ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view Worktrees # ↵ space open worktree in c"},{"url":"wiki/console/config.html","title":"Configuration","section":"canopy console","lede":"Every setting in canopy console's config.toml, with its default.","headings":[{"id":"where","text":"Where the file is"},{"id":"settings","text":"Every setting"},{"id":"look","text":"Look and feel"},{"id":"behavior","text":"Behavior"},{"id":"tools","text":"Editor, AI and GitHub"},{"id":"workspace","text":"Workspace"},{"id":"tables","text":"Keys and commands"},{"id":"keys","text":"Remapping keys"},{"id":"custom","text":"Custom commands"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"canopy console reads one file, config.toml . Every setting is optional: leave a line out and you get the default. You never have to create the file by hand, because canopy writes a starter one the first time it runs. Where the file is # The file is ~/.canopy/config.toml . On Windows it is %USERPROFILE%\\.canopy\\config.toml . Two things change that: CANOPY_HOME points canopy at a different folder instead of ~/.canopy . The config, your themes and the settings canopy desktop shares all live there. CANOPY_CONFIG points at one specific file, and wins over the folder. Not sure which file canopy is reading? canopy --config-path prints it, and canopy --example-config prints a starter file you can save there. Before 1.0.7 the folder was $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/canopy (usually ~/.config/canopy ); canopy copies it to ~/.canopy the first time it runs and leaves the old one alone. Typos. If the file can't be read, canopy starts with the defaults and shows \"Config error, using defaults\" with the line that's wrong. Fix the line and restart. See troubleshooting . Every setting # Look and feel # theme \"canopy\" Color theme: canopy , catppuccin , gruvbox , nord , light , or the name of your own theme . ⌃T cycles themes while canopy runs. See Themes . compact false Tighter layout: less padding and no gaps between panels. splash true Play the short tree animation when canopy starts. Any key skips it. nerd_font false Use Nerd Font icons. Only turn this on if your terminal font has them. mac_key_symbols macOS: true , otherwise false Show modifier keys as ⌃ ⌥ ⇧ instead of ctrl- and alt- . Behavior # teach_mode true Show the git command behind each action in the footer. T toggles it while canopy runs. confirm_destructive true Ask before discarding changes, hard resets, force deletes and similar. watch true Refresh the moment a file or git changes (an editor saving, git in another terminal). With false , canopy checks every few seconds instead. See syntax highlighting and live refresh . syntax true Color code in diffs by language: keywords, strings, comments, numbers, function and type names, in your theme's colors. log_page_size 300 How many commits History loads at a time. More load as you scroll. Editor, AI and GitHub # editor unset The editor e opens files in, for example \"code --wait\" , \"nvim\" or \"zed --wait\" . Unset means $VISUAL , then $EDITOR , then vi (Notepad on Windows). ai \"auto\" What Fix with AI ( A on a failed run in Actions) opens: auto (the first of Claude Code or Codex that's installed), claude , codex , off , or your own command line with {prompt} where the prompt goes, such as \"my-ai {prompt}\" . ai_open \"tab\" Where it opens: tab (a new tab in tmux, Windows Terminal, iTerm, kitty or WezTerm; a new window in other terminals), window , or here (canopy steps aside until it exits). gh_program unset Path to the GitHub CLI, if it isn't gh on your PATH . The CANOPY_GH environment variable does the same. Workspace # workspace_dirs [] Folders the Workspace tab scans for repositories, for example [\"~/code\"] . Empty means the folder above the repository you opened. workspace_depth 3 How many folders deep to look. Folders named node_modules , target , vendor , dist , build and Library are skipped. Keys and commands # [keys] empty Remap actions to different keys. See Remapping keys . [[custom_commands]] none Run your own commands from a key. See Custom commands . Remapping keys # Under [keys] , map an action's name to a key, or to a list of keys. The names are in the last column of the generated key list , and the command palette ( : ) finds actions by name too. [keys] toggle_stage = \"s\" commit = [\"c\", \"ctrl-s\"] push = \"alt-p\" A key is a character ( x , X , ? ) or one of these names: space , enter , esc , tab , backtab , backspace , delete , up , down , left , right , pageup , pagedown , home , end , f1 to f12 . Put ctrl- in front, or alt- , or ctrl-alt- for both (in that order). A key you assign is taken away from whatever used it before. If that leaves"},{"url":"wiki/console/cli.html","title":"Command line","section":"canopy console","lede":"The canopy command's flags, arguments and environment variables.","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Usage"},{"id":"print","text":"Options that print something and exit"},{"id":"examples","text":"Examples"},{"id":"env","text":"Environment variables"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"You start canopy console by typing canopy in a terminal. Most of the time that's all you need: run it inside a repository and it opens that repository. The options below help when you want a different folder, theme or setup. Usage # $ canopy [OPTIONS] [PATH] PATH The repository to open. Defaults to the current folder. If the folder isn't a repository yet, canopy offers to create one . -w , --workspace DIR Start on the Workspace tab, scanning DIR for repositories. -t , --theme THEME Use a theme for this run only: canopy , catppuccin , gruvbox , nord , light , or one of yours . -V , --version Print the version. -h , --help Print help. Options that print something and exit # These don't open the app. They print to the terminal, so you can save the output to a file with > . --list-themes List the built-in themes and your own, then exit. --export-theme THEME Print a theme as JSON, to start your own from. --export-settings Print the settings canopy desktop shares (theme, editor, AI assistant, teach mode) as JSON. --import-settings FILE Apply a settings file from --export-settings or canopy desktop to your config, then exit. Only those settings change. --config-path Print where canopy reads its config. --example-config Print a starter config. See configuration . Examples # $ canopy ~/code/my-project # open a repository from anywhere $ canopy -w ~/code # pick from every repository under ~/code $ canopy -t nord # try a theme for this run $ canopy --example-config > ~/.canopy/config.toml Careful with that last one. It overwrites an existing config file. Run canopy --config-path first to see where the file is, and only use it when you don't have one yet. Environment variables # CANOPY_CONFIG Read the config from this file instead of the default location. CANOPY_HOME Use this folder instead of ~/.canopy for the config, your themes and canopy desktop's shared settings. CANOPY_GH Path to the GitHub CLI. Same as gh_program in the config, which wins if both are set. VISUAL , EDITOR The editor e opens files in, for example code --wait , when editor isn't set in the config. Without either, canopy uses vi (Notepad on Windows). If something goes wrong # Nothing opens, and canopy just prints a line about itself. canopy only draws its screens when it's running in a real terminal. Started from a script or a pipe, it prints its version and how to run it, then exits. Run canopy directly in a terminal window. \"git was not found on your PATH\". canopy runs the real git, so install it and open a new terminal. See troubleshooting . \"is not a folder\". The PATH you gave doesn't exist, or is a file. Check the spelling. \"No theme called ...; using canopy\". The name in -t or your config doesn't match a theme. canopy --list-themes shows every name canopy found. A theme file with a mistake in it is skipped. Related pages # Configuration Themes Start a new repository Troubleshooting"},{"url":"wiki/console/highlighting.html","title":"Syntax highlighting and live refresh","section":"canopy console","lede":"How canopy console colors code in diffs and notices changes as you make them, and how to turn either off.","headings":[{"id":"highlighting","text":"Syntax highlighting"},{"id":"which-files","text":"Which files"},{"id":"highlighting-off","text":"Turning it off"},{"id":"live-refresh","text":"Live refresh"},{"id":"what-counts","text":"What counts as a change"},{"id":"refresh-settings","text":"Settings"},{"id":"wrong","text":"If something goes wrong"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Two things make canopy console feel alive. Syntax highlighting colors the code in a diff, so keywords, strings and comments stand out from the plus and minus lines. Live refresh means that when you save a file in your editor, or run git in another terminal, canopy notices straight away instead of waiting for you to press ⌃R . Syntax highlighting # In a diff, canopy colors the code by language: keywords, strings, comments, numbers, constants, function and type names, and (in HTML and XML) tags and attributes. The colors come from your theme, so they change when you change the theme with ⌃T (see themes ). The red and green background that marks removed and added lines stays, so you can still tell what changed. A diff shows two versions of a file at once, so canopy reads the old side (unchanged and removed lines) and the new side (unchanged and added lines) separately. That keeps a multi-line string or comment colored correctly on both sides, even when only part of it changed. Which files # canopy picks the language from the file's extension, then from its name (a Makefile has no extension). It knows the common ones, for example Rust, Python, JavaScript, Go and Makefile. If it doesn't recognize the language, the diff is shown in plain text. Nothing is broken; there's just nothing to color. Two limits keep it quick. A line longer than 2,000 characters (minified code, data) is left plain, and so is a change of more than 5,000 lines in one block (a generated file, a lock file). Binary files are never colored. Turning it off # Highlighting is on by default. To turn it off, set syntax = false in ~/.canopy/config.toml . See configuration for the file and every other setting. Live refresh # canopy watches the repository's folder. When something changes, it waits a quarter of a second for things to settle (saving a file or making a commit touches several files at once), then refreshes what's on screen. Your changed files, branch and counts update by themselves. What counts as a change # Files in your working folder that git doesn't ignore. Saving a file in your editor counts. Build output and node_modules , which your .gitignore lists (see ignore files ), don't, so a build running in the background won't make canopy flicker. Git's own state: a new commit, a switched branch, a tag, the staging area, or a merge or rebase starting or finishing. Git's internal bookkeeping (its object store, logs and lock files) is filtered out. So you can stage in canopy, commit from another terminal, edit in your editor, and all three stay in step. Settings # watch = true in config.toml is the default. With watch = false , canopy doesn't watch the folder. It still checks the status every 3 seconds, and you can refresh by hand at any time with ⌃R . If something goes wrong # A file has no colors. canopy doesn't recognize its language, the line is very long, or the change is very large (see above). The diff is still correct. Colors look wrong or washed out. That's your terminal's color support, not highlighting: see troubleshooting . A change didn't show up right away. Press ⌃R to refresh. If it keeps happening, open an issue with your operating system, your terminal and where the repository lives. Related pages # Configuration Themes A tour of canopy console Troubleshooting"},{"url":"wiki/console/troubleshooting.html","title":"Troubleshooting","section":"canopy console","lede":"Fixes for the problems people run into with canopy console.","headings":[{"id":"start","text":"Starting canopy"},{"id":"no-repo","text":"\"No repository open\""},{"id":"config","text":"\"Config error, using defaults\""},{"id":"git-missing","text":"\"git\" isn't found"},{"id":"windows","text":"Windows: \"canopy\" isn't recognized"},{"id":"github","text":"GitHub screens"},{"id":"gh","text":"The GitHub screens say gh isn't installed or logged in"},{"id":"display","text":"Keys and display"},{"id":"option","text":"Option shortcuts don't work"},{"id":"glyphs","text":"Borders or symbols look misaligned"},{"id":"colors","text":"Colors look wrong or washed out"},{"id":"windows-display","text":"Windows: odd characters or colors"},{"id":"stale","text":"A change I made elsewhere isn't showing"},{"id":"git","text":"Git problems"},{"id":"push","text":"Push was rejected, or \"your branch has diverged\""},{"id":"conflict","text":"A merge, rebase or pull stopped with conflicts"},{"id":"undo","text":"I did something I didn't mean to"},{"id":"bug","text":"Something else"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Fixes for the problems people actually run into with canopy console. If yours isn't here, the last section says how to report it. Starting canopy # \"No repository open\" # You started canopy outside a git repository, or with -w . Go to the Workspace screen ( 6 ) and press ↵ on a repository, or cd into one and run canopy again. In a folder that isn't a repository, canopy offers to create one. canopy -w ~/code starts on the Workspace screen for the repositories under a folder. If the Workspace is empty, set workspace_dirs in config.toml . \"Config error, using defaults\" # The config file has a typo. The message names the problem. canopy --example-config prints a valid starting point, and canopy --config-path prints where the file lives; see configuration . \"git\" isn't found # canopy runs the real git, so it has to be installed and on your PATH . Install it from git-scm.com (on Windows, winget install --id Git.Git -e ), then start canopy again. Windows: \"canopy\" isn't recognized # The folder with canopy.exe isn't on your PATH , or the terminal was opened before you added it. Follow the Windows steps , then open a new terminal. GitHub screens # The GitHub screens say gh isn't installed or logged in # canopy uses the GitHub CLI ( gh ) with your own login. Install it and log in, then reopen canopy: brew install gh and gh auth login (on Windows, winget install --id GitHub.cli -e , or see cli.github.com ). If gh lives somewhere unusual, set gh_program in config.toml . The git screens work without it. Keys and display # Option shortcuts don't work # canopy understands both terminals that send Option as Alt/Meta and macOS Terminal's default, where Option types a special character on a US keyboard layout. With other keyboard layouts, turn on Use Option as Meta key (Terminal → Settings → Profiles → Keyboard) or the equivalent in iTerm2 (Profiles → Keys → Left Option key: Esc+). Every key is listed on the keys page , and each has a plain-letter version that needs no Option. Borders or symbols look misaligned # canopy only draws characters that are one column wide in standard monospace fonts. If lines still don't meet, your terminal font may be missing box-drawing characters: try SF Mono, Menlo, JetBrains Mono or Fira Code. In iTerm2, turn off Treat ambiguous-width characters as double width . Colors look wrong or washed out # canopy uses 24-bit (\"true\") color. Some terminals, including older versions of macOS Terminal, don't support it and approximate the colors; iTerm2, kitty, WezTerm, Ghostty and Alacritty do support it. Trying another theme with ⌃T can help too. Diff colors come from the same theme; you can turn them off with syntax = false (see syntax highlighting ). Windows: odd characters or colors # Use Windows Terminal : the old console window (conhost) has limited color and character support. A font like Cascadia Mono (the default) shows every character canopy uses. A change I made elsewhere isn't showing # canopy usually notices right away (see live refresh ). If it doesn't, press ⌃R . If watch is false in your config, canopy only checks every 3 seconds. Git problems # Push was rejected, or \"your branch has diverged\" # Someone pushed to the branch before you. Pull first with p . If your branch has diverged (you and the remote each have new commits), pushing with P asks what to do: Pull with rebase , Pull with merge , or Force push (with lease) . Force push replaces the remote branch with yours, so use it only on a branch nobody else uses; --force-with-lease stops if someone pushed since your last fetch. See sync with a remote . A merge, rebase or pull stopped with conflicts # That's normal: two changes touched the same lines and git wants you to choose. canopy lists the conflicted files and shows what to do next. See resolve a merge conflict . To give up instead, abort the merge or rebase; that puts things back as they were before it started. I did something I didn't mean to # Press z to undo the last action, or see undo a mistake "},{"url":"wiki/console/faq.html","title":"FAQ","section":"canopy console","lede":"Short answers to common questions about canopy console.","headings":[{"id":"install","text":"How do I install and update it?"},{"id":"modifier","text":"Which key is ⌃ , and where is ⌘ ?"},{"id":"find-a-key","text":"How do I find a key?"},{"id":"undo","text":"How do I undo something?"},{"id":"teach","text":"What is teach mode?"},{"id":"replace-git","text":"Does it replace git?"},{"id":"safe","text":"Is it safe?"},{"id":"privacy","text":"Does it send my code anywhere?"},{"id":"hosts","text":"Does it work with GitLab, Bitbucket and others?"},{"id":"settings","text":"Where are my settings?"},{"id":"which-app","text":"Console or desktop?"},{"id":"license","text":"What is the license?"},{"id":"bug","text":"How do I report a bug?"},{"id":"related","text":"Related pages"}],"text":"Short answers to common questions about canopy console. For the desktop app, see its own FAQ; for anything git-shaped, start with Git in ten minutes . How do I install and update it? # canopy console runs on macOS, Linux and Windows. On macOS and Linux use Homebrew ( brew install shankar-sachin/canopy/canopy ) or the install script; on Windows use winget install shankars.canopy , the install script, or the .zip from GitHub Releases. See Get started . The command is canopy ; Command line lists every flag. To update, run brew upgrade canopy for Homebrew, winget upgrade shankars.canopy for winget, or re-run the install command you used. Which key is ⌃ , and where is ⌘ ? # The console uses ⌃ (Control) and ⌥ (Option or Alt), never ⌘ , because terminals do not pass Command through. So commit is ⌃S and notifications are ⌥N , on every system. How do I find a key? # Press ? for the help for the screen you are on, or : for the command palette, which lists everything you can do and lets you search by name. Keyboard shortcuts has the full list. How do I undo something? # Press z . It undoes the last thing canopy did, such as a commit, a merge, a rebase or a pull. For older mistakes, the Reflog tab ( 7 ) remembers where every branch has been. See Undo a mistake . What is teach mode? # A footer that shows the exact git command behind what you just did, so you learn git as you use it. Press T to turn it on or off. For a command canopy has no key for, press ! to run raw git. Does it replace git? # No. canopy console runs the real git on your machine and reads the same repositories, so anything you do in it shows up in git and the other way round. You can drop back to the command line at any time. Is it safe? # Actions that lose work ask first, and z undoes most of the rest. Everything canopy does is a normal git command, and teach mode shows each one. Git cannot restore edits you threw away with a hard reset or discard, so those ask first and say so. Merge and rebase on the Branches tab run at once (undo with z ), and force push is offered only from the diverged menu, where it is marked dangerous. See Branches, merging and rebasing and Sync with a remote . Does it send my code anywhere? # No. Git runs on your computer. The GitHub tabs use your own GitHub CLI ( gh ) login and only contact GitHub when you open one or act on it. Does it work with GitLab, Bitbucket and others? # Yes for the git parts: commits, branches, fetch, pull and push work with any remote. The PRs, Issues, Actions and Releases tabs are GitHub only. Where are my settings? # In ~/.canopy/config.toml , described in Configuration . Themes are shared with canopy desktop; see Themes . Press ⌃T to change the theme from inside the app. Console or desktop? # They work on the same repositories and share settings and themes, so you can use both. Desktop is the friendlier one to start with; console is faster once your fingers know the keys, works over SSH, and has the extras: interactive rebase, bisect, blame, worktrees and the reflog. What is the license? # canopy is free and open source under the MIT license. The code is on GitHub , and the licenses of everything it is built from are in THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt. How do I report a bug? # Open an issue with what you did, what you expected, the version, your terminal and your operating system. Pull requests are welcome; CONTRIBUTING.md has the rules. Related pages # A tour of canopy console Keyboard shortcuts Troubleshooting Configuration"},{"url":"changelog.html","title":"Changelog","section":"Product","lede":"What's new in each canopy release.","headings":[{"id":"next","text":"What's next"}],"text":"Changelog What's new. Every release, newest first. Binaries and notes are on GitHub Releases . On this page v1.0.15 v1.0.14 v1.0.13 v1.0.12 v1.0.11 v1.0.10 v1.0.9 v1.0.8 v1.0.7 v1.0.6 v1.0.5 v1.0.4 v1.0.3 v1.0.2 v1.0.1 v1.0.0 v0.4.0 v0.3.0 v0.2.0 v0.1.0 What's next v1.0.15 Latest October 2026 An audit, and straight talk about the Mac app. The Mac app will not be notarized by Apple. The docs used to say &ldquo;yet&rdquo;; now they say it plainly. Opening it the first time still takes one confirmation ( Open Anyway , or xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/canopy.app ). canopy console only opens http(s) links in the browser, like canopy desktop already did. canopy console: scrolling a very long info panel can no longer wrap around. Fixed the docs: the install script and the README said Linux x86_64 only, but Linux ARM64 has been supported all along. Audited: every Rust test, the JavaScript tests, the website, the licenses, and repositories with spaces, accents, emoji, renames, binary files and a detached HEAD. v1.0.14 September 2026 The wiki, rewritten as two guides , and a pass over the whole website. The terminal app is now called canopy console. Nothing else changes: the command is still canopy , and the Homebrew, winget and Cargo packages keep their names. Two full guides. The wiki now has a canopy desktop guide (with real screenshots) and a canopy console guide (with the keys and real screens). Each has its own sidebar, tour, recipes and reference. The git basics and themes pages are shared. The old page addresses still work: they show a short \"moved\" page with a link to each guide. Every guide and recipe has the same shape: what it is, the steps, What git runs , If something goes wrong , and related pages. New pages: a tour of each app, write good commits, sync with a remote, put work aside with the stash, ignore files, tag a version and publish a release, every desktop setting, desktop keyboard shortcuts, and syntax highlighting and live refresh. The FAQ and troubleshooting pages are written per app, and the search index is generated, so it can't go stale. The README shows both apps, and there is now a CONTRIBUTING.md : open a pull request, don't push to a branch directly. The name is written in lowercase everywhere: canopy, canopy desktop, canopy console. Fixed: the action name for the Pull requests screen in the [keys] config is goto_pull_requests (it had a space in it), and the steps lists on the wiki no longer break onto separate lines. Under the hood: scripts that retake the desktop and console screenshots, and checks that fail if a wiki page loses a section, names a key canopy console doesn't have, mixes up the two apps, or falls out of the search index. v1.0.13 September 2026 Syntax-highlighted diffs and live refresh in both apps. Diffs are colored by language : keywords, strings, comments, numbers, function and type names, in your theme's colors, in Changes, History, Stash and pull requests. Both sides of a change are read in order, so a multi-line string or comment stays right. Rust, Python, JavaScript, Go, C and C++, Java, HTML, CSS, Markdown, shell scripts and many more. In the terminal app, syntax = false in the config turns it off. Live refresh. Save a file in your editor, or commit or switch branches in another terminal, and canopy updates right away instead of on its next check. Files git ignores (build output, node_modules ) don't count. In the terminal app, watch = false goes back to checking every few seconds. Terminal app: editing a file that was already changed now updates its diff too (before, only a change in the file list did). README: a new demo recording of the terminal app, made in a throwaway repository by scripts/demo-repo.sh and demo.tape . v1.0.12 September 2026 A full audit of both apps and the website, with many more tests. Both apps refuse a branch, tag, remote or stash name that starts with a dash (like -D ), which git would read as an option instead of a name. Dashes inside a name are fine. Desktop: after you switch repositories, the GitHub pages no longer show the last repository's pull request or issue, and Home's GitHub card and failure banner are for the right repository straight away. Desktop: a branch or tag you create from History shows up in the list right away, and History updates when a branch moves. Desktop: if Connect and push fails (say the repository isn't on GitHub yet), trying again works instead of saying the remote already exists. The setup steps also keep what you typed after an error. Terminal app: going back a hunk ( N or [ ) works from a hunk's first change, where going forward puts you. Before, it stayed put. Terminal app: selecting lines in an empty diff, or one that just got shorter, can't crash canopy. Themes: two custom themes whose names differ only in punctuation ( My Theme and My-Theme ) no longer overwrite each other, and choosing one by name picks the right one. Website: search results for words like lt or amp display correctly, and Downloads marks the newest full release (not a pre-release) as the latest. Under the hood: tests for the desktop app's pages, a website audit (links, anchors, page titles, the search index), and scripts/audit.sh , which runs them all before each release. v1.0.11 September 2026 Bug fixes across both apps, from a scan of the whole codebase. Checking out a name that is also a file (say a branch called notes when there's a file notes ) could throw away your unsaved edits to that file. Now git only ever reads it as a branch or commit, in both apps. Undo after a rebase (or pull --rebase ) now goes back to before the rebase, instead of one step inside it, which changed nothing. Terminal app: tags and branches with non-Latin names (like リリース ) no longer crash canopy when you check them out, tag or reset. History of every branch works in a repository with no commits yet, and on a new branch that has none. Unstaging a file when git hits an unrelated error no longer stages the file's deletion instead. Windows: links with & in them open correctly in your browser, and the default editor opens files whose path has an & . Settings: an editor or AI command with a tab or line break in it no longer breaks config.toml ; ~ on its own works in workspace_dirs ; theme colors like #+12345 are rejected. v1.0.10 September 2026 Notifications, one step away in both apps. Desktop app: a Notifications page in the sidebar, with a count of unread ones there and on Home. It shows every repository or just this one, unread or all, and says why you got each. Open one in canopy (pull requests and issues in this repository), view it on GitHub, mark it read, or mark all read. Terminal app: ⌥N opens your notifications from any tab, and the header and Home show how many are unread. ] on Issues still works. v1.0.9 September 2026 Getting the Mac app ready for notarization, and a quieter terminal app. Desktop app (macOS): canopy now declares that it opens Terminal (the Apple Events permission Apple requires), and macOS asks for it early, the first time canopy starts while Terminal is open, instead of in the middle of Fix with AI. If macOS blocks canopy from opening Terminal or iTerm, Fix with AI and terminal editors now say so and point to the setting that allows it , instead of claiming a window opened. The terminal app no longer suggests the desktop app: the startup tip, ⌥D and the desktop_tip setting are gone. Desktop app: now and then (at most every two weeks) it mentions canopy for the terminal, if you don't have it. Turn it off in Settings → General. Terminal app: started without a terminal (by a script or an installer's check), canopy prints what it is and exits instead of failing. Windows builds include the C runtime, so canopy.exe runs on a clean machine. v1.0.8 September 2026 One canopy, two ways to use it. canopy desktop is where to start. canopy for the terminal does everything, from the keyboard. Nothing is renamed: the canopy command, Homebrew and winget packages stay th"},{"url":"desktop.html","title":"canopy desktop","section":"Product","lede":"canopy desktop: the friendly way to start with git and GitHub, on Mac, Windows and Linux. Release preview.","headings":[{"id":"preview","text":"A release preview"},{"id":"install","text":"Install"},{"id":"learning","text":"Made for learning"},{"id":"home","text":"Home"},{"id":"changes","text":"Changes"},{"id":"history","text":"History"},{"id":"github","text":"GitHub"},{"id":"fix-with-ai","text":"Fix with AI"},{"id":"new-repo","text":"Start a new repository"},{"id":"settings","text":"Settings & updates"},{"id":"windows","text":"Windows"},{"id":"keys","text":"Keys"},{"id":"more","text":"Ready for more? canopy console"}],"text":"Start here · Desktop · release preview canopy desktop New to git? This is the one to install. canopy desktop shows your repository in a window on macOS, Windows or Linux, explains what everything means, and gives you a button for the next step. Stage single lines with a click, browse the commit graph, and work with pull requests, issues and Actions, with a safety net the whole way. On this page Release preview Install Made for learning Home Changes History GitHub Fix with AI New repository Settings & updates Windows Keys A release preview Not notarized by Apple. The Mac app isn't signed with an Apple Developer ID or notarized, and it won't be. The first time you open it, macOS asks you to confirm: open System Settings → Privacy & Security and click Open Anyway , or run xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/canopy.app . Linux builds don't need any of this. The desktop app runs the real git and GitHub CLI on your machine, exactly like canopy console. It never sends your code anywhere; the only thing it asks the internet for is the latest version number, once a day (you can turn that off). Install Every installer, including older versions, is on the Downloads page. macOS, with Homebrew $ brew install --cask shankar-sachin/canopy/canopy-desktop That puts canopy.app in your Applications folder. Update it later with brew upgrade --cask canopy-desktop . Or download the .dmg for Apple silicon or Intel from the latest release . Linux Each release has a .deb (Debian, Ubuntu and friends) and an AppImage (any distribution) for x86_64: $ sudo apt install ./canopy-desktop-*-amd64.deb # or $ chmod +x canopy-desktop-*-x86_64.AppImage && ./canopy-desktop-*-x86_64.AppImage From source $ git clone https://github.com/shankar-sachin/canopy.git && cd canopy $ cargo run --release -p canopy-desktop -- ~/code/your-repo On Linux this needs the WebKitGTK development packages ( libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev ). canopy desktop needs git ; the GitHub pages also use the GitHub CLI . Made for learning Next steps with buttons. Home says in plain words where your repository stands (changes to commit, commits to push, a merge in progress) and gives you a button for each step, including Connect to GitHub… when a repository isn't backed up anywhere yet. Every term explained. Hover over Staged, Changed, Conflicts, Stashes, Branches or Remotes (or click its ? ) to see what it means. The ↑ and ↓ counts explain themselves too. A short introduction the first time: change, stage, commit, push, with links to Git in ten minutes . Dismiss it, and bring it back from Settings → General. A safety net. canopy asks before anything that throws work away and says exactly what would be lost. After a commit, checkout, merge or reset, it reminds you that ⌘Z undoes it. Learn git as you go. With Show git commands on (the default), every action shows the command canopy ran. Home Where your branch stands, what's staged and changed, and next steps with a button for each: commit, push, pull, resolve. If this branch has a pull request, its checks and review are right there. Changes Staged, changed and conflicted files on the left; the diff on the right. Stage a whole file, a hunk, or just the lines you click (shift-click for a range). Diffs are colored by language, and the list updates by itself when you save a file in your editor ( how that works ). Write the message below and commit with ⌘↵ ; the commits guide covers what makes a good one. Conflicts get keep-mine / take-theirs buttons and a banner to continue or abort the merge. History The commit graph, drawn properly: merges, branches and tags. Search messages (or @name for an author), see any commit's full diff, and check out, branch, tag, cherry-pick, revert or reset from there. Branches and Stash have pages of their own ( what the stash is for ), and Undo ( ⌘Z ) reverses the last commit, reset or checkout. GitHub Pull requests with their checks, reviews and comments: check out, review, merge, comment, or open a new one from your bran"},{"url":"download.html","title":"Downloads","section":"Docs","lede":"Download canopy desktop and canopy console for macOS, Windows and Linux: every installer, for every version.","headings":[],"text":"Downloads Every installer, every version. canopy desktop and canopy console, for macOS, Windows and Linux. Take the latest, or pick an older version. Your system comes first. Desktop app canopy console Version Loading… Loading the releases from GitHub… This page lists the files from canopy's GitHub releases . Every release has them all. Each file has a .sha256 next to it, to check the download. Everything comes straight from GitHub releases . Setting up for the first time? See Get started and installing the desktop app ."},{"url":"features.html","title":"canopy console","section":"Product","lede":"canopy console: git and GitHub, everything from the keyboard. A tour of every tab, with real screenshots.","headings":[{"id":"home","text":"1 · Home"},{"id":"changes","text":"2 · Changes"},{"id":"history","text":"3 · History"},{"id":"conflicts","text":"Conflicts"},{"id":"refs","text":"4 · Branches, tags, remotes, worktrees & submodules"},{"id":"stash","text":"5 · Stash and 7 · Reflog"},{"id":"workspace","text":"6 · Workspace"},{"id":"undo","text":"Undo"},{"id":"bisect","text":"Bisect"},{"id":"look","text":"Colors, themes and live refresh"},{"id":"palette","text":"Command palette & teach mode"}],"text":"canopy console Everything, from the keyboard. This is canopy's power tool: every git and GitHub action one key away, and the place new power features land first. It has ten tabs. Press their number to jump straight there, or tab to cycle. Every screenshot here is a real frame from the app. On this page Home Changes History Conflicts Branches & more Stash & Reflog Workspace Undo Bisect Colors & live refresh Palette & teach mode New to git? Start with canopy desktop : the same repository view in a window, with buttons, plain-language explanations and undo. Come back here when you want more power. It keeps teach mode, next steps and the wiki . 1 · Home Your repository at a glance: the branch and whether it's in sync, what's staged and changed, recent commits with a graph, commit activity, and your branches. Next steps reads the state of the repo and tells you, in plain words, what to do next and which key does it. canopy — Home 2 · Changes Everything you've changed, grouped as conflicts, changes, and staged. space stages or unstages a file, a does all of them, d discards (after telling you exactly what you'll lose). Press ↵ to step into the diff. There, space stages just the line under the cursor , v selects a range, and ↵ takes the whole hunk. s switches to a side-by-side view. canopy — Changes When you're ready, c opens the commit dialog, with a live length guide for the summary line. A amends the last commit instead. 3 · History The commit graph for your branch, with branch and tag labels. / filters by message, author or hash. On any commit: check it out, cherry-pick C , revert t , reset r (soft, mixed or hard), tag T , branch from it n , or fold staged changes into it F . canopy — History i opens a visual interactive rebase from the selected commit: reorder with J / K , and pick, squash, fixup, edit or drop each commit. L on a file shows its history (following renames) and B shows blame, where B again steps back to the version before that line changed. canopy — Blame Conflicts When a merge or rebase stops on conflicts, the header says so and Home tells you what to do. Open a conflicted file with ↵ and resolve it one conflict at a time: o keeps yours, t takes theirs, b keeps both. The file is written as you go and staged when nothing's left; u puts the markers back if you change your mind. Then C continues the merge or rebase. canopy — Conflicts 4 · Branches, tags, remotes, worktrees & submodules One tab, five views: [ and ] switch between them. Branches: local and remote, with ahead/behind. Checkout, create, rename, delete, merge M , rebase r , set upstream. Tags: create ( name: message makes an annotated tag), push, delete here or everywhere. Remotes: add, rename, change URL, fetch one, remove. Worktrees: check out another branch in its own folder, open it in canopy, remove or prune. Submodules: see which are in sync, update one or all, open one. canopy — Branches 5 · Stash and 7 · Reflog Stash shelves your uncommitted work ( S from anywhere) so you can switch tasks; apply, pop or drop it later ( the stash, step by step ). Reflog lists everywhere HEAD has been, including commits you \"lost\" to a reset. Reset back to any entry from there. 6 · Workspace Point canopy at your code folders ( workspace_dirs in the config, or canopy -w ~/code ) and see every repository at once: branch, uncommitted changes, conflicts, and whether it's ahead or behind. ↵ opens one; F fetches them all. canopy — Workspace Undo z undoes the last thing that moved your branch, using git's reflog ( more on undoing mistakes ). It tells you what it's about to do first: After a commit : the commit is undone and its changes come back staged . After a checkout : you switch back to where you were. After a reset, merge or rebase : the branch moves back, and your uncommitted changes are kept. Bisect Something broke, but you don't know when. In History, select a commit where it still worked and press b . Git checks out a commit halfway between; test it, press b , an"},{"url":"get-started.html","title":"Get started","section":"Docs","lede":"Install canopy: the desktop app to start with, or canopy console with Homebrew, winget, a script or Cargo.","headings":[{"id":"brew","text":"Install with Homebrew"},{"id":"script","text":"Install without Homebrew"},{"id":"cargo","text":"Install with Cargo"},{"id":"source","text":"Run from a clone of the source"},{"id":"windows","text":"Windows"},{"id":"first","text":"First run"},{"id":"config","text":"Configuration"},{"id":"themes","text":"Themes"},{"id":"update","text":"Updating"}],"text":"Get started Up and running in a minute. canopy comes in two apps, both for macOS, Linux and Windows. They need git ; GitHub features also use the GitHub CLI . On this page Homebrew Install script With Cargo From source Windows First run Configuration Themes Updating Recommended if you're new canopy desktop A friendly app for macOS, Windows and Linux, with buttons, explanations and undo. brew install --cask shankar-sachin/canopy/canopy-desktop on a Mac, winget install shankars.canopy-desktop on Windows. Everything, from the keyboard canopy console The power tool: every git and GitHub action one key away. Install it below with Homebrew, winget, a script or Cargo, then run canopy in any repository. You can use both: they share themes and settings in ~/.canopy . Every installer for both apps, in every version, is on Downloads . The rest of this page is about canopy console; the desktop app has its own page . Install with Homebrew The easiest way on a Mac, and on Linux if you use Homebrew there. It installs a prebuilt binary, so nothing is compiled: $ brew install shankar-sachin/canopy/canopy Then run canopy inside any git repository. Install without Homebrew On macOS or Linux (x86_64 or ARM64), this script downloads the latest release, checks its SHA-256, and puts canopy in ~/.local/bin . It doesn't need sudo. Read it first if you like: scripts/install.sh . $ curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/shankar-sachin/canopy/main/scripts/install.sh | sh A specific version: … | sh -s -- v1.0.0 . Somewhere else: CANOPY_INSTALL_DIR=~/bin . To remove it: … | sh -s -- --uninstall (your config stays). Install with Cargo If you have Rust installed ( rustup.rs ), Cargo can build and install canopy straight from GitHub. It lands in ~/.cargo/bin , which rustup already put on your PATH : $ cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/shankar-sachin/canopy canopy-git-tui Why canopy-git-tui ? That's the package name (the name canopy was already taken on crates.io). The command it installs is still canopy . Run from a clone of the source To try the latest code, or hack on it: $ git clone https://github.com/shankar-sachin/canopy.git $ cd canopy # run it without installing (the -- passes arguments to canopy) $ cargo run --release -- ~/code/your-repo # or install your local build as `canopy` $ cargo install --locked --path crates/canopy cargo run builds and starts canopy in one step; the first build takes a minute or two, later ones are quick. Without a path after -- , canopy opens the repository you're in. Since you're in the canopy clone itself, that's a handy way to try it on a real history. Running the tests: cargo test --workspace . Windows On Windows, canopy console runs in Windows Terminal (recommended) and in PowerShell. Homebrew is for macOS and Linux, so on Windows install it one of these ways. With winget winget installs canopy (x64 or Arm), puts canopy on your PATH , and installs git too if you don't have it: PS> winget install shankars.canopy Update later with winget upgrade shankars.canopy . For the GitHub tabs, also install and log in to the GitHub CLI: PS> winget install --id GitHub.cli -e PS> gh auth login Open a new terminal, then run canopy inside any repository. If winget doesn't find the package yet (new versions take a few days to be approved), use the install script below. 1. Or: install git (and optionally the GitHub CLI) PS> winget install --id Git.Git -e PS> winget install --id GitHub.cli -e # only for the GitHub tabs PS> gh auth login 2. Install canopy The install script downloads the latest release, checks it, installs it to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\canopy , and adds that folder to your PATH . It doesn't need admin rights ( read it ): PS> irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/shankar-sachin/canopy/main/scripts/install.ps1 | iex Open a new terminal, then run canopy . Or do it by hand: 2a. Download canopy Every release includes canopy-<version>-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.zip , and from 1.0.1 canopy-<version>-aarch64-pc-wind"},{"url":"github.html","title":"GitHub","section":"Product","lede":"Pull requests, issues, CI runs and releases in canopy, through the GitHub CLI. Your login, your permissions. Keys shown are for canopy console.","headings":[{"id":"setup","text":"Setup"},{"id":"prs","text":"8 · Pull requests"},{"id":"line-comments","text":"Comment on a line"},{"id":"issues","text":"9 · Issues"},{"id":"notifications","text":"Notifications"},{"id":"actions","text":"0 · Actions"},{"id":"logs","text":"Read the whole log"},{"id":"releases","text":"Releases"},{"id":"fix-with-ai","text":"Fix with AI"},{"id":"home","text":"On Home"},{"id":"privacy","text":"What canopy sends"}],"text":"GitHub Pull requests, issues & CI. canopy talks to GitHub through the official GitHub CLI, gh . Your login, your permissions, nothing new to trust. This page walks through canopy console's keys; canopy desktop has the same pages as buttons, and the GitHub flow guides for canopy desktop and canopy console walk through it. On this page Setup Pull requests Line comments Issues Notifications Actions CI logs Releases Fix with AI On Home What canopy sends Setup GitHub features need gh , logged in. Nothing gets installed for you; if gh is missing or logged out, the GitHub tabs show exactly what to run. $ brew install gh $ gh auth login That's it. Open canopy in any repository whose remote is on github.com. 8 · Pull requests Open pull requests with their checks ( ✓ 2/2 , ✗ 1 failed ), review state, author, and age. f cycles between open, yours, review requested, and all. The side panel shows the description, every check, reviews, and comments; D adds the diff, with review comments shown under the lines they're about. canopy — Pull requests space Check out the pull request's branch n Open a pull request for the current branch (push it first with P ) r Review: approve, comment, or request changes C Comment on the pull request D Show the diff, with review comments inline ↵ then C In the diff: comment on the line under the cursor M Merge: merge commit, squash, or rebase (warns when checks fail) X Close o Open in your browser Comment on a line Press D to show the diff, ↵ to move into it, and put the cursor on the line you mean. Press C , write the comment, and press ⌃S . It shows up on GitHub as a review comment on that line. Comments on removed lines go on the old side of the diff. Existing comments appear right under their lines, with the author and when they wrote it. 9 · Issues Open, assigned-to-you, or all issues, with labels and comment counts. Read the full discussion in the side panel, then n to file one, C to comment, X to close (or reopen a closed one). Notifications Press ⌥N from any tab (or ] on Issues) for your GitHub inbox. The header shows how many are unread, in every repository, and so does Home. It has review requests, mentions, assignments and CI results, newest first, with unread ones marked ● . f switches between this repository and everywhere, and u between unread and all. ↵ / o Open it in your browser m Mark read M Mark all read (this repository, or everywhere) canopy — Issues 0 · Actions Workflow runs for your branch ( f for all branches), refreshing every few seconds while any run is in progress. Select a failed run to see which job and step failed, and the end of its log, cleaned up so you can read it. R re-runs the failed jobs. Read the whole log Press L on a finished run to open its full log. Failed jobs come first and the viewer starts on the first error. Timestamps, color codes and runner noise are stripped. / Search (ignores case; matches are highlighted) n / N Next / previous match j k , ⌃D ⌃U Scroll a line, or a page g / G Top / end esc Close Releases Press ] on Actions to see releases: tag, title, date, and whether each is a draft, pre-release or the latest. The side panel shows the notes and the files attached. n creates a release. canopy suggests the next tag (after v1.2.0 comes v1.2.1 ), and if you leave the notes empty, GitHub writes them from the merged pull requests. canopy — Actions Fix with AI When a run fails, canopy can hand the problem to your AI coding assistant. Press A on a failed run in Actions (in canopy desktop: Fix with Claude Code on the run, or on the Home banner that appears when your latest commit fails). The prompt is written from the failure: the workflow, the commit, which job and step failed, GitHub's summary (including why a workflow file is invalid, when GitHub couldn't run it at all) and the end of the log, cleaned up. It goes in .git/canopy/fix-ci.md , so it's never committed. It opens your assistant in a new terminal tab (tmux, Windows Terminal, iTerm, kitty or WezTerm) or wi"},{"url":"index.html","title":"canopy","section":"Home","lede":"canopy makes git and GitHub easy to see and safe to use: a friendly desktop app to start with, and canopy console that does everything from the keyboard.","headings":[],"text":"1.0.8 One canopy, two ways to use it → Git, at a glance. canopy shows your whole repository in one place and tells you what to do next. Start with the friendly desktop app. When you want every git and GitHub action one key away, there's canopy console. Desktop Terminal Curl Homebrew $ brew install --cask shankar-sachin/canopy/canopy-desktop For macOS, with Homebrew. Wrong OS? Browse installers here → Start here: canopy desktop canopy console Desktop app canopy console canopy — Home Screenshots of the real apps, not mockups. Everything git does One screen for the whole repository. What changed, what's staged, where your branch stands, and what to do next, in both apps. The ones marked Console are in canopy console, where new power features land first. Stage single lines Open any diff, then stage or unstage a line, a range, or a hunk. Craft commits that say exactly one thing. Undo anything Undo the last commit, reset, merge, or checkout ( ⌘Z in the desktop app, z in the terminal). An undone commit comes back as staged changes. Conflicts, one at a time See ours and theirs for each conflict. Keep one, the other, or both. The file is staged once nothing's left. History that makes sense A commit graph with search in both apps. In the terminal, also file history that follows renames, blame, and a visual interactive rebase. Bisect, guided Console Mark a commit that worked, answer good or bad at each step, and canopy points at the commit that broke things. Every repo, one view Console The Workspace tab shows every repository in your code folders: branch, changes, and sync state. GitHub built in Pull requests, reviews, issues, and CI runs, through the official GitHub CLI and your own login. Yours to configure Themes you can make yourself, shared by both apps. In the terminal, also remappable keys, custom commands on any key, and a palette that finds every action by name. Two ways to use canopy Start in a window. Grow into the keyboard. Both apps run the real git and GitHub CLI on your computer, with your own login. They share themes and settings in ~/.canopy , so you can use both. Start here canopy desktop The friendly one. For your first commit, and for every day after it. Next steps say, in plain words, where you are and give you a button for what comes next. Every term explained: hover over (or click ? on) staged, changed, ahead and behind to see what they mean. Asks before anything destructive, and says exactly what would be lost. Undo takes back the last commit, reset or checkout with ⌘Z . Shows the git command behind every action, so you learn git as you go. Get canopy desktop Everything, from the keyboard canopy console The power tool. Every git and GitHub action one key away, and new power features land here first. Line-level staging, interactive rebase, fixups, cherry-picks, bisect and worktrees. Every repository at once in the Workspace tab, plus notifications, releases and inline PR reviews. Vim-style keys you can remap, custom commands on any key, and a command palette. Real git underneath: your hooks, config, signing and credential helpers all just work. A single small binary that starts instantly. Teach mode and next steps are still there when you want them. Tour canopy console # both apps show what they ran. You press space on a line in a diff: $ git apply --cached --unidiff-zero --whitespace=nowarn - # stdin: a patch with just the selected lines # you undo a commit: $ git reset --soft 1f3a9c2 ← what that just ran Roadmap Where canopy is growing. Built in the open, one phase at a time. Read the changelog → v0.1 The git dashboard v0.2 Remappable keys, tags & remotes, conflicts one by one v0.3 GitHub PRs, issues & CI · blame, bisect, worktrees v0.4 The canopy wiki, the logo, and a roomier TUI v1.0 Notifications, releases, inline reviews, CI logs · Windows & winget v1.0.2 canopy desktop for macOS & Linux (preview) v1.0.3 canopy desktop on Windows 11 · no-compiler Homebrew v1.0.4 Start a new repository, HTTPS or SSH explained"},{"url":"keys.html","title":"Console keys","section":"Docs","lede":"Every key in canopy console, on every screen, generated from the app itself. canopy desktop's shortcuts are on the wiki.","headings":[{"id":"k-global","text":"Everywhere"},{"id":"k-status","text":"Changes"},{"id":"k-diff","text":"Diff panel (after ↵)"},{"id":"k-conflict","text":"Conflict panel"},{"id":"k-log","text":"History"},{"id":"k-branches","text":"Branches"},{"id":"k-tags","text":"Tags"},{"id":"k-remotes","text":"Remotes"},{"id":"k-worktrees","text":"Worktrees"},{"id":"k-submodules","text":"Submodules"},{"id":"k-stash","text":"Stash"},{"id":"k-workspace","text":"Workspace"},{"id":"k-reflog","text":"Reflog"},{"id":"k-pulls","text":"Pull requests"},{"id":"k-issues","text":"Issues"},{"id":"k-runs","text":"Actions"},{"id":"k-releases","text":"Releases"},{"id":"k-notifications","text":"Notifications"}],"text":"Reference Every key. Generated from canopy's own key tables, so it always matches the app. Screen keys take priority over the global ones. Remap anything with the [keys] table in your config, using the names in the last column. These are canopy console's keys; canopy desktop's shortcuts are on the desktop shortcuts page. On this page Everywhere Changes Diff panel Conflict panel History Branches Tags Remotes Worktrees Submodules Stash Workspace Reflog Pull requests Issues Actions Releases Notifications Keys are shown the Mac way: ⌃ is Control, ⌥ is Option (Alt on other keyboards), ⇧ is Shift. Option shortcuts work in any terminal, including macOS Terminal's default setup where Option types special characters. ⌃Q and ⌥Q quit from anywhere, even inside a dialog. In canopy itself, Linux shows ctrl-q / alt-q instead; set mac_key_symbols in the config to choose. Everywhere ? ⌥H f1 help help : ⌃P ⌃K command palette palette q ⌃Q ⌥Q ⌃C quit quit 1 ⌥1 go to home goto_home 2 ⌥2 go to changes goto_changes 3 ⌥3 go to history goto_history 4 ⌥4 go to branches goto_branches 5 ⌥5 go to stash goto_stash 6 ⌥6 go to workspace goto_workspace 7 ⌥7 go to reflog goto_reflog 8 ⌥8 go to pull requests goto_pull_requests 9 ⌥9 go to issues goto_issues 0 ⌥0 go to actions goto_actions tab ⌥→ ⌃→ next tab next_screen ⇧tab ⌥← ⌃← previous tab prev_screen ⌃R ⌥R refresh refresh ⌃L redraw the screen redraw c ⌥C ⌃S commit commit f ⌥F fetch fetch p ⌥P pull pull P ⌥⇧P push push z ⌥Z undo last action undo S ⌥S stash changes stash_push b bisect: find the commit that broke something bisect ! ⌥G run git command raw_git T toggle teach mode toggle_teach ⌃T ⌥T cycle theme cycle_theme ⌥N notifications: your GitHub inbox, from any tab show_notifications k ↑ up up j ↓ down down ⌃U pageup page up page_up ⌃D pagedown page down page_down g home top top G end bottom bottom esc back back Changes space stage/unstage toggle_stage a stage/unstage all stage_all d discard changes discard ↵ l open enter A amend last commit amend e open in editor open_editor i add to .gitignore ignore o resolve: keep ours take_ours t resolve: take theirs take_theirs C continue merge/rebase continue_op X abort merge/rebase abort_op L history of this file file_history B blame: who changed each line blame Diff panel (after ↵) space stage/unstage line(s) stage_line ↵ stage/unstage hunk stage_hunk v select range range_select n ] next hunk next_hunk N [ previous hunk prev_hunk s toggle split view toggle_side_by_side h ← back back L history of this file file_history B blame: who changed each line blame C comment on this line of a pull request line_comment Conflict panel o keep ours for this conflict keep_ours t take theirs for this conflict keep_theirs b keep both (ours, then theirs) keep_both j ↓ n next conflict next_conflict k ↑ N previous conflict prev_conflict u restore conflict markers restore_conflict e open in editor open_editor h ← back back History ↵ l open enter space checkout commit checkout_commit C cherry-pick cherry_pick t revert commit revert r reset to commit reset_menu i interactive rebase from here rebase_interactive n new branch here branch_from_commit T tag commit tag_commit F fixup staged into commit fixup_commit y copy hash copy_hash / search search Branches space ↵ checkout checkout n new branch new_branch d delete branch delete_branch M merge into current merge r rebase current onto rebase R rename branch rename_branch u set upstream set_upstream / search search ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view Tags space ↵ checkout tag checkout_tag n new tag at HEAD new_tag d delete tag delete_tag P push tag push_tag ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) next_refs_view [ previous view (branches/tags/remotes) prev_refs_view / search search Remotes f fetch this remote fetch_remote n add remote add_remote d remove remote remove_remote e change remote URL edit_remote_url R rename remote rename_remote ] next view (branches/tags/remotes) ne"}];
